window.MOSHION_DOCS = [{"slug":"overview","title":"Overview","pages":[{"title":"What is moSHion?","body":"moSHion is a beginner-friendly 2D game engine for the web. It pairs a simple drawing canvas with real Box2D physics (via planck.js), so you can draw shapes and make them bump into each other without wiring the two worlds together yourself.\n\nIt's aimed at people who've written a little JavaScript and want to make a game this afternoon — not a week from now. The whole API is a handful of globals: Sprite, Group, world, camera, kb, mouse.\n\nYou don't import anything. You don't install anything. You write two functions — setup and draw — and the engine runs them for you. Add an update function if you need per-frame input or logic.","code":"let player;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n  player = new Sprite(200, 100, 40, 40);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n  new Sprite(200, 380, 400, 20, 'static');\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"The sketch lifecycle","body":"A moSHion sketch is made of up to five functions with special names. You never call them yourself: moSHion finds each one by its name and runs it at the right moment. You always need setup(). The other four are optional, and a sketch without one of them still runs.\n\nEach one has ONE job:\npreload() runs ONCE, BEFORE setup(), and only if your game loads files: loadSound(), loadJSON() and loadImage() go here, and the game waits until every file is ready. Most sketches do not need it (\"Getting files ready\", page 4).\nsetup() runs ONCE, at the very start. Make the canvas and your sprites here.\nupdate() runs EVERY FRAME, BEFORE physics. The rules of your game go here: keys, movement, jumping, touching, score.\ndraw() runs EVERY FRAME, AFTER physics. The picture BEHIND your sprites goes here: background() first, then any shapes or words.\ndrawTop() runs EVERY FRAME, LAST, on top of everything. The score, lives and other words that must stay readable go here. The camera is off while it runs, so (10, 20) is always near the top-left corner.\n\nEvery frame happens in this order, about 60 times a second:\n1. your update() runs,\n2. the physics step moves every sprite: gravity, velocity and collisions all happen here,\n3. your draw() runs,\n4. moSHion paints the sprites, on top of whatever draw() drew,\n5. your drawTop() runs, on top of the sprites.\n\nDo you need update()? No. A sketch with only setup() and draw() works, and the course starts that way. Moving the rules into update() changes just one thing: a velocity you set there moves the sprite in this frame, while the same line in draw() waits for the next frame. At 60 frames a second you cannot see that. The real reason to use it is a sketch that reads in halves: the rules in update(), the picture in draw().\n\nOne rule is not optional: drawing goes in draw() or drawTop(), never in update(). update() runs first, so the background() at the top of draw() paints over anything update() drew.\n\nframeCount tells you how many frames have passed since the sketch started, which is handy for timing things without a clock. \"Where does my code go?\", the next page, sorts the usual lines into the functions.","code":"let player, ground;\nlet jumps = 0;\n\n// ONCE, at the start: the canvas and your sprites\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n  player = new Sprite(200, 100, 30, 30);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n  ground = new Sprite(200, 380, 400, 20, 'static');\n}\n\n// EVERY FRAME, BEFORE physics: the rules (keys, movement, score)\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -4;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 4;\n  else                           player.vel.x = 0;\n\n  if (kb.presses(' ') && player.colliding(ground)) {\n    player.vel.y = -7;\n    jumps = jumps + 1;\n  }\n}\n\n// EVERY FRAME, AFTER physics: the picture BEHIND the sprites\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  fill('gold');\n  circle(340, 70, 50);   // a sun, behind everything\n}\n\n// EVERY FRAME, LAST: words ON TOP of everything\nfunction drawTop() {\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(16);\n  text('Jumps: ' + jumps, 10, 24);\n  text('frame: ' + frameCount, 10, 44);\n}"},{"title":"Where does my code go?","body":"Not sure which function a line belongs in? Look up what the line does.\n\nIn preload(), once, before setup(), only if your game loads files:\nloadSound('jump.mp3'), loadJSON('levels.json'), loadImage('hero.png'). The game waits for them, so setup() can use them.\n\nIn setup(), once:\nnew Canvas(400, 400).\nThe sprites and groups you start with: new Sprite(), new Group().\nSettings that start the game: world.gravity.y = 10, player.color, a collider.\nThe first value of a variable made with let at the top of the file.\n\nIn update(), every frame, before physics:\nReading the keys and the mouse: kb.pressing(), kb.presses(), mouse.presses().\nMoving things: player.vel.x = 4, player.speed, moveTowards().\nChecking touches: player.collides(enemy), player.overlaps(coins, ...).\nChanging the score, the lives, a timer, or the level.\nMaking or deleting a sprite because something happened: a bullet when space is pressed, a coin that was picked up.\n\nIn draw(), every frame, after physics:\nbackground(), as its first line.\nShapes and words that belong BEHIND the sprites: a sun, a floor pattern, a sign the player walks in front of.\n\nIn drawTop(), every frame, last:\nThe score, lives, a timer, \"Game over\": words that must sit on top of every sprite and stay put when the camera moves.\n\nA quick test for any line: if it CHANGES the game, it goes in update(). If you only LOOK at it, it goes in draw() or drawTop(). Behind the sprites is draw(), in front of them is drawTop().\n\nNever in update() or draw(): new Canvas() and the sprites you start with. Those two run 60 times a second, so you would make 60 new sprites every second. A sprite made because something happened (the bullet above) is different: it is made once, on the frame it happens.","code":"let player, coins;\nlet score = 0;\n\n// setup(): the canvas, the sprites you start with, the settings\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  player = new Sprite(200, 200, 30, 30);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n\n  coins = new Group();\n  coins.diameter = 18;\n  coins.color = 'gold';\n  coins.collider = 'none';\n  new coins.Sprite(120, 120);\n  new coins.Sprite(300, 260);\n  new coins.Sprite(260, 320);\n}\n\n// update(): keys, movement, touching, score\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -4;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 4;\n  else                           player.vel.x = 0;\n  if (kb.pressing('up'))         player.vel.y = -4;\n  else if (kb.pressing('down'))  player.vel.y = 4;\n  else                           player.vel.y = 0;\n\n  player.overlaps(coins, (p, coin) => {\n    score = score + 1;\n    coin.delete();\n  });\n}\n\n// draw(): background first, then the picture behind the sprites\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  fill('#333');\n  rect(0, 340, 400, 60);   // a floor stripe, under the sprites\n}\n\n// drawTop(): the score, on top of everything\nfunction drawTop() {\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(16);\n  text('Score: ' + score, 10, 24);\n  if (score === 3) text('All the coins!', 10, 48);\n}"},{"title":"Getting files ready: preload()","body":"A file takes a moment to arrive. Load a sound, a picture or a JSON file in setup() and the game starts without waiting: the sound is not ready yet, the picture's size is 0, and the JSON is still empty.\n\npreload() fixes that. Write it above setup() and load your files in it:\npreload() runs once, before setup(). The game waits until every file it asked for is ready, then runs setup().\nIn setup() everything is there: a picture has its real width and height, the JSON's data is filled in, and sound.loaded is true.\nOnly three loaders wait: loadSound(), loadJSON() and loadImage(). Called anywhere else, they load in the background, as always.\n\nIt waits exactly as long as the files need, no more. A small file is ready in a blink and the game starts at once. A big file on a slow connection takes longer; after a moment the page says \"Loading... 2 of 3 files\" until it is done.\n\nA file that cannot load (a wrong name, a missing file) does not stop the game. The console says which file and why, and the game starts without it. If no file finishes for 10 seconds, the page says which file it is still waiting for and shows a Start anyway button. Press it to play without that file.\n\nOne thing preload() cannot change: browsers keep sound off until the player clicks or presses a key. A sound that is ready still cannot play in setup(), and moSHion says so in the console. Play sound effects on an event (a key, a hit). Music started with .loop() waits and begins at the first click.\n\nloadImage(url) is new: it loads a picture by its address and gives it back, ready for sprite.image. For the built-in pictures you can also just write sprite.texture = 'apple'.","code":"let jump, apple, player;\n\n// ONCE, before setup(): the files this game needs\nfunction preload() {\n  jump = loadSound('jump');\n  apple = loadImage('textures/apple.png');\n}\n\n// ONCE, when every file is ready\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n  // the picture has its real size already, so the sprite can match it\n  player = new Sprite(200, 100, apple.width * 2, apple.height * 2);\n  player.image = apple;\n  new Sprite(200, 290, 400, 20, 'static');\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.presses(' ')) {\n    player.vel.y = -6;\n    jump.play();   // on an event, so the browser lets it play\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}\n\nfunction drawTop() {\n  fill('white');\n  text('apple: ' + apple.width + ' x ' + apple.height + ' px. Press space.', 10, 24, 16);\n}"},{"title":"Global mode","body":"moSHion runs in \"global mode.\" That means Canvas, Sprite, world, kb, and friends are already on the page — you just use them. No import, no new Engine(), no setup boilerplate.\n\nThe same rule applies to preload, setup, update, draw and drawTop. When you define a top-level function with one of those names, the engine finds it and calls it at the right time (the sketch lifecycle, page 2, says when).\n\nTo share a sprite between setup and update, declare a let at the top of your file and assign to it inside setup. If you use const inside setup, the variable is trapped there and update can't see it.","code":"let player;\nlet ground;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n  player = new Sprite(200, 100, 30, 30);\n  player.color = 'gold';\n  ground = new Sprite(200, 380, 400, 20, 'static');\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.presses(' ') && player.colliding(ground)) {\n    player.vel.y = -8;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Debugging your sketch","body":"When something goes wrong, open your browser's developer console (F12 on most systems). Errors show up there with a line number. console.log(value) is your best friend — sprinkle it anywhere to see what's happening.\n\nThree mistakes trip up almost every beginner. First: forgetting background() in draw. The canvas never clears, so old frames pile up and everything looks smeared. Second: creating new Sprite() inside draw or update instead of setup. You'll spawn a new sprite every frame and the sketch will grind to a halt. Third: using a variable before it's assigned — usually because you wrote let player inside setup instead of at the top of the file.\n\nIf a sprite isn't showing up, log its x and y. If input doesn't work, log kb.pressing('left') inside update to confirm the key name.","code":"let player;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  player = new Sprite(200, 200, 30, 30);\n  console.log('setup ran, player at', player.x, player.y);\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.presses(' ')) {\n    console.log('space pressed on frame', frameCount);\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"}]},{"slug":"canvas","title":"Canvas","pages":[{"title":"Drawing plain shapes","body":"Not everything on screen has to be a sprite. Inside draw() you can paint a shape straight onto the canvas: rect(x, y, w, h), square(x, y, size), circle(x, y, diameter) and line(x1, y1, x2, y2). These have no physics and nothing collides with them; they are just pixels, redrawn every frame after background().\n\nrect and square are placed by their top-left corner. circle is placed by its centre, the same way a sprite is.\n\nfill(color) sets the colour inside the next shapes, and stroke(color) sets the outline. noFill() and noStroke() turn either one off, and strokeWeight(n) makes the outline thicker. They stay set until you change them, so set the colour just before the shape it belongs to. square() follows fill() like every other shape: fill('deepskyblue') then square(...) draws a blue square.\n\nA colour can be a name or string, fill('deepskyblue'), or numbers as in p5: fill(80, 250, 123) for green, fill(200) for grey. For a see-through colour add an alpha: fill(0, 0, 0, 120), where the alpha runs from 0 (clear) to 255 (solid), or the string fill('rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5)'), where it runs from 0 to 1.","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n\n  noStroke();\n  fill('deepskyblue');\n  square(40, 60, 80);\n\n  fill('tomato');\n  circle(220, 100, 80);\n\n  noFill();\n  stroke('gold');\n  strokeWeight(4);\n  rect(280, 60, 80, 120);\n  line(40, 240, 360, 240);\n}"},{"title":"Creating the canvas","body":"new Canvas(width, height) inside setup() makes the drawing surface for your sketch. Without it, nothing shows up. Call it first, before you make any sprites, so the engine knows how big the world is.\n\nThe canvas coordinates start at (0, 0) in the top-left. X grows to the right, y grows downward. A 400 by 400 canvas has its center at (200, 200).\n\nThe default background is transparent — you'll usually replace it by calling background() inside draw.","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#202030');\n}"},{"title":"frameCount","body":"frameCount is a global number that starts at 0 and goes up by 1 every frame. It's the simplest way to animate: use it to offset a position, wrap a counter with %, or trigger something every N frames.\n\nCombine frameCount with Math.sin or Math.cos to get smooth back-and-forth motion without any physics.\n\nA game runs 60 frames a second on every screen, including 120 Hz and 144 Hz monitors, so frameCount, vel.x and every() mean the same thing on any computer. A slow computer can still fall below 60.\n\nframeRate() (no arguments) returns how many frames per second your sketch is actually managing right now — call it inside draw() and print it if you want to see whether a busy scene is slowing things down.","code":"let ball;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  ball = new Sprite(200, 200, 40);\n  ball.collider = 'none';\n  ball.color = 'gold';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  ball.x = 200 + Math.sin(frameCount / 30) * 120;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(14);\n  text('frame ' + frameCount, 10, 20);\n}"},{"title":"Time in seconds: deltaTime","body":"deltaTime is how many milliseconds passed since the last frame: about 16.7 when the game runs at 60 frames a second, more when the computer is slow. Divide by 1000 to get seconds.\n\nThat lets you write timing in seconds instead of counting frames. x += 120 * deltaTime / 1000 moves 120 pixels every second, and a cooldown can be a number of seconds that counts down by deltaTime / 1000 each frame, so 2.5 means two and a half seconds.\n\nCounting frames still works too (frameCount, every(), 60 frames = 1 second). Use whichever reads more clearly for what you are making.","code":"let cooldown = 0;   // seconds until you can dash again\nlet x = 40;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 200);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  let seconds = deltaTime / 1000;\n  x += 60 * seconds;            // 60 pixels a second\n  if (x > 380) x = 40;\n\n  if (cooldown > 0) cooldown -= seconds;\n  if (kb.presses('space') && cooldown <= 0) {\n    x += 80;\n    cooldown = 1.5;             // one and a half seconds\n  }\n\n  fill('gold');\n  circle(x, 100, 30);\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(16);\n  text(cooldown > 0 ? 'Dash in ' + cooldown.toFixed(1) + ' s' : 'Dash: READY (space)', 10, 24);\n}"},{"title":"Fresh dice: random() and randomSeed()","body":"random() with nothing in the brackets gives a number from 0 up to (not including) 1. random(10) gives one from 0 up to 10, random(5, 10) one from 5 up to 10, and random(['red', 'gold', 'lime']) picks one item from a list.\n\nEvery time you press Run, the numbers are different, so each playthrough deals a new level, a new enemy wave, a new set of stars.\n\nTo get the same numbers every run, call randomSeed(n) at the top of setup() with any whole number. The same seed always gives the same sequence. That helps when you are hunting a bug that only happens with one particular layout: seed it, and the layout comes back every time.","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  // randomSeed(7); // uncomment: the same stars every run\n  for (let i = 0; i < 40; i++) {\n    const star = new Sprite(random(400), random(300), random(2, 6));\n    star.collider = 'none';\n    star.color = random(['white', 'gold', 'lightblue']);\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#112');\n}"},{"title":"Background and clearing","body":"background(color) paints over the whole canvas. Call it first thing in draw() so last frame's pixels are erased before new ones go down. Skip it and you'll see comet-like trails behind every moving sprite.\n\nA solid color takes a name ('black', 'tomato'), a hex string ('#222', '#ff00aa'), or an rgba string. For a see-through background use 'rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)' — only 10% opaque, so old frames fade out gradually instead of vanishing.\n\nThat fade trick is how you get motion trails without tracking history yourself. The lower the alpha, the longer the trail.","code":"let p;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  p = new Sprite(200, 200, 20);\n  p.collider = 'none';\n  p.color = 'gold';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  p.x = 200 + Math.cos(frameCount / 20) * 150;\n  p.y = 200 + Math.sin(frameCount / 20) * 150;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)');\n}"},{"title":"Math helpers: abs, sqrt, lerp, dist and friends","body":"These work the way they do in p5 and in plain math class. abs(n) drops a minus sign: abs(-4) is 4. sqrt(n) is the square root: sqrt(9) is 3. pow(base, exponent) raises to a power: pow(2, 3) is 8. ceil(n) rounds up, floor(n) rounds down, and int(n) is the same as floor(n), so int(3.7) is 3 and int(-3.7) is -4. max(...) and min(...) pick the biggest or smallest of the numbers you give them, or of one list: max(3, 9, 2) is 9.\n\natan(v) is the angle whose tangent is v, in radians (in degrees after angleMode(DEGREES)). ellipse(x, y, w, h) draws an oval w wide and h tall, and with no h it is a circle w across.\n\nlerp(a, b, t) is the number a fraction t of the way from a to b: lerp(0, 10, 0.25) is 2.5. dist(x1, y1, x2, y2) is the straight-line distance between two points, and it also takes two things with an x and a y, so dist(a, b) works on two sprites: dist(0, 0, 3, 4) is 5.\n\nnf(num, left, right) turns a number into text with a fixed number of digits, which keeps a score from jumping around on screen. left is how many digits go before the point and right is how many after: nf(7, 2) is '07', nf(3.14159, 1, 2) is '3.14', and nf(12.5, 3, 1) is '012.5'.\n\nmillis() is the number of milliseconds since the sketch started. TWO_PI is a full turn in radians and HALF_PI is a quarter turn, next to PI. These are for radians; sprites turn in degrees unless you call angleMode().","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 200);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background(30);\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(16);\n  text('dist(0, 0, 3, 4) = ' + dist(0, 0, 3, 4), 20, 40);\n  text('lerp(0, 10, 0.25) = ' + lerp(0, 10, 0.25), 20, 70);\n  text('int(-3.7) = ' + int(-3.7), 20, 100);\n  text('nf(7, 2) = ' + nf(7, 2), 20, 130);\n  text('seconds so far = ' + nf(millis() / 1000, 1, 1), 20, 160);\n}"},{"title":"Vectors: createVector","body":"A vector is a pair of numbers, x and y, with some maths built in. createVector(x, y) makes one (new Vector(x, y) does the same). It is handy for a position or a speed you want to add up.\n\nv.add(x, y), v.sub(x, y), v.mult(n) and v.div(n) CHANGE v and also hand it back, so you can chain them. v.set(x, y) replaces both numbers. v.copy() makes a separate vector, so changing the copy leaves v alone. v.mag() is its length: createVector(3, 4).mag() is 5. v.normalize() shrinks it to length 1 while keeping its direction, and v.dist(other) is the distance to another point.\n\nBecause add, sub, mult, div and normalize change the vector they are called on, call copy() first when you want to keep the original.","code":"let v;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 200);\n  v = createVector(3, 4);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background(30);\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(16);\n  let w = v.copy().mult(2);\n  text('v = (' + v.x + ', ' + v.y + '), length ' + v.mag(), 20, 50);\n  text('copy times 2 = (' + w.x + ', ' + w.y + ')', 20, 90);\n  text('v is still (' + v.x + ', ' + v.y + ')', 20, 130);\n}"}]},{"slug":"coordinates","title":"Coordinates","pages":[{"title":"Where is (0, 0)?","body":"By default, (0, 0) is the top-left corner of the canvas. x counts to the right and y counts DOWN, so a bigger y is lower on the screen. The browser's own canvas works this way, and so does p5play. These are called screen coordinates, and every sketch uses them unless you ask for something else.\n\nIf you are used to graphs from math class, where y goes up, this can feel upside down. Two helpers show you what is going on. showGrid(50) draws a light grid with a line every 50 pixels and the axes labelled, so you can see exactly where (0, 0) is and which way y grows. showMouse() writes the pointer's (x, y) right next to it. Call both from draw(), every frame. They only draw: nothing collides with the grid.\n\nMove the mouse over the canvas and read the numbers: y gets bigger as you move down.","code":"let box;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  box = new Sprite(100, 50, 40, 40, 'static');\n  box.color = 'deepskyblue';\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  showGrid(50);\n  showMouse();\n}"},{"title":"Math mode: centre, y up","body":"Put world.origin = 'math' on the line right after new Canvas() and the canvas becomes the graph from math class. (0, 0) is the CENTRE of the canvas, x grows to the right and y grows UP. A positive rotation turns counter-clockwise, the way math measures angles from the +x axis.\n\nEvery number keeps its meaning but points the math way. vel.y = 5 moves a sprite up. direction = 90 points up. Gravity pulls toward smaller y, so a falling world is world.gravity.y = -10, with a minus sign, because down is negative now. The camera starts at (0, 0). Text and pictures still read the right way up, and showGrid() and showMouse() label the math numbers.\n\nSet it first: before you make any sprite and before you set gravity. The engine will not move things that already exist, so setting it too late stops the sketch with an error that tells you to move the line up. Any value other than 'math' or 'screen' is an error too. 'screen' is the default, so you never need to write it.\n\nIn this example the ball starts 100 above the centre and falls onto the floor at y = -120.","code":"let ball, floor;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  world.origin = 'math';\n  world.gravity.y = -10;\n\n  ball = new Sprite(0, 100, 30);\n  ball.color = 'tomato';\n  ball.bounciness = 0.6;\n\n  floor = new Sprite(0, -120, 300, 20, 'static');\n  floor.color = 'gray';\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  showGrid(50);\n  showMouse();\n}"},{"title":"Math mode: what else changes","body":"A tile map still reads top to bottom: the first string is the top row, and each row below it has a smaller y. new Walls() still calls the wall above the screen walls.top (its y is the bigger one) and the wall below it walls.bottom. A ground sensor under a player 40 tall is addSensor(0, -21, 26, 4), because down is negative now.\n\nrect(x, y, w, h) covers x to x + w and y to y + h, so from its corner it grows right and UP. text(str, x, y) puts the bottom of the letters on y, and the letters sit above it, still the right way up.\n\ndrawTop() and camera.off() still draw in a frame that ignores the camera, but that frame is centred and y-up too: on a 400 by 300 canvas, text('Score', -190, 130) sits near the top-left corner. mouse.x and mouse.y are math numbers as well, so the mouse and the grid always agree.\n\nDrive the player with the arrow keys and watch the camera follow. The grid moves with the world, so its labels always say where the player really is.","code":"let walls, level, player;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  world.origin = 'math';\n\n  walls = new Group();\n  walls.collider = 'static';\n  walls.color = 'gray';\n\n  level = new Tiles([\n    '#########',\n    '#       #',\n    '#   #   #',\n    '#       #',\n    '#########',\n  ], -160, 80, 40, 40, { '#': walls });\n\n  player = new Sprite(-80, 0, 24, 24);\n  player.color = 'gold';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('right'))     player.vel.x = 3;\n  else if (kb.pressing('left')) player.vel.x = -3;\n  else                          player.vel.x = 0;\n\n  if (kb.pressing('up'))        player.vel.y = 3;\n  else if (kb.pressing('down')) player.vel.y = -3;\n  else                          player.vel.y = 0;\n\n  camera.x = player.x;\n  camera.y = player.y;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  showGrid(40);\n}\n\nfunction drawTop() {\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(14);\n  text('x: ' + round(player.x) + '  y: ' + round(player.y), -190, 135);\n}"}]},{"slug":"sprite","title":"Sprite","pages":[{"title":"Your first sprite","body":"A Sprite is the basic building block of any moSHion game. Every sprite has a position, a size, and (usually) a physics body that moves and collides automatically.\n\nnew Sprite(x, y, width, height) creates a rectangular sprite centered on (x, y). With no extra argument it's dynamic, meaning gravity pulls it and it bumps into other sprites. Gravity starts at 0, so this one stays where you put it until you set world.gravity.y.\n\nStore the sprite in a variable declared outside setup() so update() and draw() can reach it later. Without a reference, you can't move it or check its state.","code":"let player;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  player = new Sprite(200, 180, 40, 40);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Collider types: dynamic, static, kinematic, none","body":"Every sprite has a physics type. You set it by passing a string as the last argument to new Sprite, or later with sprite.collider = '...'.\n\n'dynamic' is the default — gravity pulls it, other sprites push it. Use it for players, enemies, projectiles.\n\n'static' never moves and never reacts. Use it for floors, walls, and platforms. 'kinematic' moves only when you set its velocity — gravity ignores it. Use it for moving platforms and doors. 'none' turns physics off entirely — the sprite still draws, but nothing collides with it. Use it for cursors, UI, and decorations. A sprite with collider = 'none' is a sensor: you can still detect overlaps, but there's no physical pushback.\n\nThe type works on circles too: new Sprite(x, y, 30, 'static') is a static ball. Capital letters are fine ('Static'), and so is the first letter alone ('s'). A misspelt type such as 'statik' stops the sketch with an error that lists the four real ones, instead of quietly making a dynamic sprite. sprite.body and sprite.physicsType are older names for the same setting.","code":"let elevator;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n\n  new Sprite(200, 100, 40, 40);                    // dynamic (default)\n  new Sprite(200, 380, 400, 20, 'static');         // static floor\n  new Sprite(100, 260, 80, 10, 'static');          // static platform\n\n  elevator = new Sprite(320, 260, 60, 10, 'kinematic');\n  elevator.vel.y = -1;\n\n  const hint = new Sprite(200, 30, 10, 10);\n  hint.collider = 'none';\n  hint.color = 'gold';\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Position, rotation, scale","body":".x and .y are the center of the sprite, not the top-left corner. A sprite at (200, 200) has its middle at (200, 200).\n\n.rotation is in degrees. 90 is a quarter turn, 180 is a half turn, 360 is a full turn.\n\n.scale uniformly grows or shrinks how the sprite is drawn. The physics body stays the size you created it at, so very large scale values can look strange when the sprite collides.","code":"let box, spinner;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  box = new Sprite(120, 200, 40, 60);\n  box.rotation = 30;\n\n  spinner = new Sprite(280, 200, 40, 40);\n  spinner.collider = 'none';\n  spinner.color = 'gold';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  spinner.rotation += 3;\n  spinner.scale.x = 1 + 0.3 * Math.sin(frameCount / 20);\n  spinner.scale.y = spinner.scale.x;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Color, visibility, and layer","body":".color sets the fill. You can use CSS names ('red'), hex ('#ff8800'), or rgb strings.\n\n.stroke is the outline color and .strokeWeight is its thickness in pixels. Set strokeWeight to 0 for no outline.\n\n.visible = false hides a sprite without removing it — physics still runs. .layer controls draw order — higher layer values draw on top.\n\n.opacity fades a sprite from 0 (invisible) to 1 (fully solid, the default) — good for a hit-flash, a fade-out before delete(), or a ghosted preview. .debug = true draws the sprite's actual physics collider as a lime outline, which is the fastest way to check whether the shape you're seeing matches the shape you're colliding with.","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  const back = new Sprite(180, 200, 120, 120);\n  back.color = '#4455aa';\n  back.layer = 0;\n\n  const front = new Sprite(220, 200, 120, 120);\n  front.color = 'gold';\n  front.stroke = 'black';\n  front.strokeWeight = 3;\n  front.layer = 1;\n  front.opacity = 0.6;\n\n  const ball = new Sprite(340, 200, 40);\n  ball.color = 'tomato';\n  ball.debug = true; // outlines the real collider, useful while tuning shapes\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Shape options","body":"moSHion picks the collider shape from the constructor arguments. Two numbers make a 50×50 square: new Sprite(x, y). Three numbers make a circle: new Sprite(x, y, diameter). Four numbers make a rectangle: new Sprite(x, y, w, h).\n\nYou can also pass a body type as the last argument: new Sprite(x, y, w, h, 'static').\n\nnew Sprite() with no numbers at all makes a 50×50 square in the middle of what the camera shows.\n\nYou can change the size later: .w and .h (or .width and .height, or .diameter for a circle) resize the sprite AND its collider, so what you see is what bumps into things. On a circle, setting either .w or .h sets both, because a circle has one size. .scale is different: it only changes how the sprite is drawn.\n\nYou can read .shape to check whether a sprite is 'circle' or 'rect', and set it to switch ('box' means the same as 'rect').","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n\n  new Sprite(90, 120, 40, 60);\n\n  const ball = new Sprite(200, 120, 40);\n  ball.color = 'tomato';\n\n  new Sprite(200, 380, 400, 20, 'static');\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Removing and cleaning up sprites","body":"sprite.delete() removes a sprite from the world. After deleting, it stops drawing, stops updating, and its physics body is gone. It also removes itself from every group it belongs to, including the implicit allSprites group. There is no undo — if you only want to hide a sprite, set .visible = false instead.\n\ngroup.remove(sprite) only unparents the sprite from that group — the sprite keeps existing, drawing, and running physics. These are NOT the same operation.\n\nWhen you delete sprites while looping through a Group, iterate a copy with [...group]. Otherwise the array shrinks under your feet and you'll skip items.","code":"let bullets;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  bullets = new Group();\n  bullets.color = 'gold';\n  bullets.diameter = 10;\n  bullets.collider = 'none';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (frameCount % 10 === 0) {\n    const b = new bullets.Sprite(20, 200);\n    b.vel.x = 4;\n  }\n  for (const b of [...bullets]) {\n    if (b.x > 400) b.delete();\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(14);\n  text('Live bullets: ' + bullets.length, 14, 24);\n}"},{"title":"Angular velocity","body":"sprite.angularVelocity is the rotation speed in degrees per frame. Positive spins clockwise, negative spins counter-clockwise.\n\nYou can read it to see how fast a sprite is spinning, or write it to give a sprite a spin kick — like a pendulum that gets pushed on a mouse click. The value is in degrees per frame, matching the facade's pixel-per-frame convention for linear velocity.","code":"let rod, anchor;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n\n  anchor = new Sprite(200, 80, 12, 12, 'static');\n  rod = new Sprite(200, 180, 12, 160);\n  rod.color = 'khaki';\n\n  new HingeJoint(anchor, rod);\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (mouse.presses()) {\n    rod.angularVelocity = 6;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Speed, direction, and moveTowards","body":".speed and .direction are another way to read and set .vel, in polar form instead of x/y. .speed is how fast the sprite is moving (pixels per frame, always 0 or positive — a negative speed just points direction the other way). .direction is the heading in degrees, where 0 points right, 90 points down, matching .rotation's convention.\n\nSetting .speed keeps the current direction; setting .direction keeps the current speed. That makes \"turn without slowing down\" or \"speed up without turning\" a one-line change instead of redoing the vector math.\n\nsprite.moveTowards(x, y, tracking) nudges a sprite toward a point without touching physics at all — each call moves it tracking (default 0.1, so 10%) of the remaining distance. Call it once per update() frame for a smooth chase that naturally slows down as it arrives.","code":"let hunter, target;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  hunter = new Sprite(60, 200, 30);\n  hunter.color = 'tomato';\n  hunter.collider = 'none';\n\n  target = new Sprite(340, 200, 16);\n  target.color = 'gold';\n  target.collider = 'none';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  target.x = 200 + Math.cos(frameCount / 40) * 150;\n  target.y = 200 + Math.sin(frameCount / 40) * 150;\n  hunter.moveTowards(target.x, target.y, 0.05);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Chasing, fleeing, and pulling","body":"Two questions come first: how far away is it, and which way is it? sprite.distanceTo(other) gives the straight-line distance in pixels, and sprite.angleTo(other) gives the direction in degrees, measured the same way as sprite.rotation. Both take a pair of numbers or anything with x and y — another sprite, or mouse.\n\nmoveTowards has three relatives that work the same way, and all four accept either a pair of numbers or a target with .x and .y — so enemy.moveTowards(player, 0.02) is a complete chase.\n\nmoveTowards(target, tracking) closes that fraction of the remaining gap each call. moveAway(target, repel) is its mirror: it opens the gap by the same fraction. Both set position directly, so they ignore physics — the sprite arrives no matter what is in the way.\n\nattractTo(target, force) and repelFrom(target, force) work through physics instead, applying a real force in newtons toward or away from the point. The sprite accelerates, keeps its momentum, and bounces off whatever it hits on the way. Use these when the pull should feel like gravity or magnetism rather than a leash.\n\nrotateTowards(target, tracking) turns to face a point over several frames by setting a spin speed — unlike rotateTo, which steps or snaps the angle directly. Like rotateTo, it always turns the short way round.\n\nPassing mouse itself (not mouse.x and mouse.y) is worth doing: the sprite then ignores the cursor until it has actually moved over the canvas, instead of lunging at the top-left corner on the first frame.","code":"let hunter, prey, magnet;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  prey = new Sprite(200, 120, 18);\n  prey.color = 'gold';\n  prey.collider = 'none';\n\n  hunter = new Sprite(60, 120, 22);\n  hunter.color = 'tomato';\n  hunter.collider = 'none';\n\n  magnet = new Sprite(200, 300, 20);\n  magnet.color = 'deepskyblue';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  // prey runs from the cursor, hunter chases the prey\n  prey.moveAway(mouse, 0.04);\n  hunter.moveTowards(prey, 0.03);\n  hunter.rotateTowards(prey, 0.2);\n\n  // the blue one is pulled by physics, so it overshoots and swings back\n  magnet.attractTo(mouse, 30);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(13);\n  text('move the mouse', 14, 22);\n}"},{"title":"rotateTo","body":"sprite.rotateTo(angle, speed) turns a sprite toward angle, in degrees, using the shorter direction. Pass speed to cap how many degrees it turns per call — call it every update() frame and it eases into the target angle instead of snapping. Leave speed out and it snaps immediately, same as setting .rotation directly.\n\nIt returns a Promise, so await sprite.rotateTo(90) works if you like that style — but the rotation itself already happened by the time you'd await it, so most lesson code just calls it and moves on.","code":"let turret;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  turret = new Sprite(200, 200, 60, 16);\n  turret.color = 'slategray';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  const dx = mouseX - turret.x, dy = mouseY - turret.y;\n  const angleToMouse = Math.atan2(dy, dx) * 180 / Math.PI;\n  turret.rotateTo(angleToMouse, 4); // turns at most 4°/frame toward the mouse\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Degrees for trig: angleMode()","body":"Every angle on a sprite is in degrees: rotation, direction, angleTo(). But cos(), sin() and tan() start out in radians, the unit math uses, where a half turn is PI (about 3.14) instead of 180. So cos(90) is not 0.\n\nCall angleMode(DEGREES) once at the top of setup() and they switch to degrees: cos(90) is 0, sin(90) is 1, and atan2(y, x) gives its answer back in degrees. Now the angle a sprite faces goes straight into cos() and sin(): the example below fires a dot the way a spinning turret points.\n\nangleMode(RADIANS) switches back, and angleMode() with nothing tells you which one is on.","code":"let turret;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  angleMode(DEGREES);\n  turret = new Sprite(200, 150, 40, 12, 'static');\n  turret.color = 'gold';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  turret.rotation += 2;\n  if (frameCount % 20 === 0) {\n    const shot = new Sprite(turret.x, turret.y, 8);\n    shot.collider = 'none';\n    shot.vel.x = cos(turret.rotation) * 4;\n    shot.vel.y = sin(turret.rotation) * 4;\n    shot.life = 60;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Images and emoji on sprites","body":"sprite.image = 'player.png' loads an image from a URL and draws it in place of the default colored shape. The image stretches to fill the sprite's width and height.\n\nIf the string has no dot in it, moSHion treats it as an emoji: sprite.image = '🧍' draws that emoji character centered on the sprite at the sprite's height as the font size. This is a quick way to get a visible character without loading assets.\n\nSetting .image clears any active animation (sprite.texture), and vice versa — they're mutually exclusive.","code":"let player;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  player = new Sprite(200, 200, 64, 64);\n  player.collider = 'none';\n  player.image = '🦄';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.x -= 3;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.x += 3;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Image files on sprites","body":"sprite.image = 'assets/star.webp' gives a sprite a picture from a file path, resolved against the runner's own URL — so a path like assets/star.webp finds the file in this repo's assets/ folder no matter which page hosts the preview.\n\nA string with no dot in it is not a path but an emoji placeholder: sprite.image = '🦄' draws the character itself, sized to the sprite, no file needed. The two forms are mutually exclusive — setting one clears the other.\n\nReading sprite.image gives back what you set. Setting it also clears any animation, the same way .ani and .image already clear each other: a sprite is either a colored shape, a picture, or an animation, never two at once.\n\nFor a strip of frames, addAni(name, sheetUrl, frameCount) still slices a horizontal strip into an animation — see the animation section. A single still image is the one-frame case: draw it once, save it as one frame, or just use .image.\n\nImages stretch to the sprite's w/h, whatever their natural pixel size. Pixel art scales up and stays crisp only if the browser is told not to smooth it; this engine smooths, so prefer art at (or near) the size you will display it — assets/star.webp at 270x264 on a 40x40 sprite will look soft rather than blocky.","code":"let player, stars;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  player = new Sprite(200, 300, 48, 48);\n  player.collider = 'none';\n  player.image = 'assets/star.webp';\n\n  stars = new Group();\n  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    const c = new stars.Sprite(60 + i * 70, 120, 32, 32);\n    c.collider = 'none';\n    c.image = 'assets/star.webp';\n  }\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.x -= 3;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.x += 3;\n\n  for (let i = stars.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n    if (player.overlaps(stars[i])) stars[i].remove();\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Textures: named pixel art","body":"sprite.texture = 'coin' picks art by NAME instead of by URL. moSHion ships a catalog of 105 pixel-art textures, so you can give a sprite a picture without finding, hosting, or spelling a file path.\n\n94 are stills; 11 are animated sprite sheets that simply play when you assign the name (see below). The terrain, things, battle, numbers and candy groups are newer: ground to build levels from, flags and switches, swords and shields, the digits 0-9 for a score or a die, and candy.\n\nThe names, by group —\n\ncharacters: player, playerBlue, playerPink, playerYellow, playerBeige, runnerJump, playerWalk, playerBlueWalk, playerPinkWalk, playerYellowWalk, playerBeigeWalk, runner\n\npickups: coin, coinSilver, coinBronze, star, heart, heartHalf, gem, gemGreen, gemYellow, gemOrange, key, keyGreen, keyBlue, keyOrange\n\nblocks: crate, crateX, block, door, torch, bomb, mushroom, mushroomBrown, cactus, exitSign\n\ncreatures: slime, slimeBlue, bee, bat, ghost, fish, fishPink, bird, snail, ladybug, worm, frog, monster, batFly, beeFly, fishSwim, ladybugWalk, snailCrawl\n\nterrain: grass, sand, snow, stone, brick, brickTan, water, waterDeep, lava, lavaDeep, ice, spikes, tree, bush, rock\n\nthings: flag, flagGreen, flagRed, flagBlue, sign, signArrow, lock, lockGreen, spring, lever, apple\n\nbattle: sword, swordGold, shield, shieldSilver, shieldGold, blaster, burst, burstGreen, burstOrange, burstBlue\n\nnumbers: num0, num1, num2, num3, num4, num5, num6, num7, num8, num9\n\ncandy: candyOrange, candyGreen, candyYellow, swirl, swirlMint\n\nCall textureNames() to print the list from inside a sketch, and hasTexture('coin') to check one name. A name that does not exist warns in the console and suggests near matches rather than failing silently, so a typo tells you what it was.\n\nNames ending in Walk, Fly, Swim or Crawl are animated sprite sheets, and so are runner (a 6-frame run cycle) and runnerJump. Assigning one plays it with no addAni call. To switch between standing and walking, swap the name when the state changes: if (player.texture !== 'playerWalk') player.texture = 'playerWalk'. Only assign when it differs, because every assignment restarts the animation.\n\nReading sprite.texture gives back the texture itself. Its name is sprite.texture.name, so ask if (s.texture.name === 'coin'). An animated texture is controlled through the same name: sprite.texture.frameDelay is the speed (game frames per picture, lower is faster), sprite.texture.frame is the picture showing now, sprite.texture.loop = false plays it once and stops on the last picture (sprite.texture.loop = 3 plays it three times), and sprite.texture.frameCount says how many pictures it has. A sprite with no texture reads back null. An animation made with addAni() counts too: after addAni, sprite.texture is the animation showing, and sprite.texture = 'run' switches to the sprite's own animation called 'run', like changeAni('run'). Setting .texture clears .image and .ani, exactly the way those two already clear each other.\n\nSetting the texture a sprite already shows does nothing, so door.texture = 'openDoor' inside update() keeps the animation playing instead of starting it over every frame. Setting a different name starts that one at its first picture. To play the same one again from the start, set sprite.texture.frame = 0. A group takes it too: coins.texture = 'coin' dresses every coin that exists and every one you spawn later.\n\nTextures draw with smoothing turned off, so a 21x21 texture stretched across a 50x50 sprite stays blocky instead of blurring. A plain .image still smooths. Textures are square, so give the sprite a roughly square shape; a very tall or wide sprite stretches the picture.\n\nIn code, saveTexture('myGuy', dataUrl) stores a data: URL under a name, and from then on sprite.texture = 'myGuy' finds it. A saved name beats a built-in one, so saving 'coin' replaces the built-in coin. Pass { frames: 4 } as a third argument and the picture is read as a horizontal strip of 4 frames, which sprite.texture = 'myGuy' then plays without an addAni call. deleteTexture('myGuy') forgets it. Keep saved art small: the store holds 64 KB per value and 512 KB in total.\n\nMost of the built-in art is Kenney's \"Platformer Art: Pixel Redux\" pack, released under CC0 — public domain, free for classroom and commercial use alike. runner and runnerJump are original to moSHion.","code":"let player, coins;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  player = new Sprite(200, 300, 48, 48);\n  player.collider = 'none';\n  player.texture = 'playerWalk'; // an animated sprite sheet\n\n  coins = new Group();\n  coins.texture = 'coin';\n  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    const c = new coins.Sprite(60 + i * 70, 120, 32, 32);\n    c.collider = 'none';\n  }\n\n  console.log('textures available:', textureNames().length);\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.x -= 3;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.x += 3;\n\n  for (let i = coins.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n    if (player.overlaps(coins[i])) coins[i].remove();\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Patterns: ground that repeats","body":"A picture on a big sprite stretches, and a 460-pixel-wide ground stretched from one small image smears. sprite.pattern repeats the picture at its natural size instead, starting from the sprite's top-left corner, so the ground looks like many tiles side by side.\n\nIt takes a URL (or a <canvas> / <img> you drew), and a group takes it too: platforms.pattern = 'tile.png' covers every platform, each repeating from its own top-left corner. If the picture fails to load the sprite keeps its plain color rather than disappearing.\n\nAn .image, .texture or animation on the same sprite draws instead of its pattern. Use pattern for surfaces and texture or image for characters and objects.\n\nThe example builds its tile in code with a <canvas>, so it needs no file.","code":"let ground, tile;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n\n  // A 16x16 brick tile drawn on a canvas.\n  tile = document.createElement('canvas');\n  tile.width = tile.height = 16;\n  const g = tile.getContext('2d');\n  g.fillStyle = '#7a4a2b';\n  g.fillRect(0, 0, 16, 16);\n  g.fillStyle = '#5a3320';\n  g.fillRect(0, 7, 16, 2);\n  g.fillRect(7, 0, 2, 7);\n  g.fillRect(0, 9, 2, 7);\n\n  ground = new Sprite(200, 270, 400, 48, 'static');\n  ground.pattern = tile;\n\n  new Sprite(200, 20, 30, 30);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Collider constants: STATIC, DYNAMIC, KINEMATIC","body":"Instead of the words in quotes you can use constants: s.collider = STATIC is the same as s.collider = 'static'. DYNAMIC is 'dynamic' and KINEMATIC is 'kinematic'. STA, DYN and KIN are shorter spellings of the same three. They are only names for the same strings, so use whichever reads best to you."},{"title":"Other names for sprite properties","body":"Some sprite properties have a second name, because code written for other libraries uses it. They all work and they do the same thing. Setting a size one resizes the collider too.\n\nsprite.pos is also sprite.position. sprite.vel is also sprite.velocity. sprite.collider is also sprite.body and sprite.physicsType. sprite.color is also sprite.colour and sprite.fill. sprite.textColor is also sprite.textFill. sprite.angularVelocity is also sprite.rotationSpeed. sprite.drag is also sprite.rollingResistance. sprite.prevPos is also sprite.previousPosition and sprite.prevRotation is also sprite.previousRotation.\n\nFor size: sprite.w is also sprite.width, sprite.h is also sprite.height, sprite.hw and sprite.halfWidth are half the width, sprite.hh and sprite.halfHeight are half the height, and sprite.d, sprite.r and sprite.radius are the diameter or radius of a circle.\n\nMost of these are marked as old names in the editor. Pick the short one you were taught and stick with it."},{"title":"Physics switches on a sprite","body":"These are properties you set to change how one sprite takes part in physics.\n\nsprite.gravityScale multiplies the world's gravity for this sprite only: 0 makes it weightless and 2 makes it fall twice as hard. sprite.rotationDrag slows its spinning the way drag slows its moving. sprite.surfaceSpeed turns a static sprite into a conveyor belt: anything touching it is carried along at that speed, in pixels per frame.\n\nsprite.isSuperFast = true switches on extra careful collision checking for a sprite so fast that it would otherwise pass straight through a thin wall. sprite.physicsEnabled = false leaves the sprite drawn but takes it out of the physics completely, and sprite.visualOnly is the same switch the other way round (sprite.visualOnly = true means sprite.physicsEnabled = false).\n\nsprite.allowSleeping lets a sprite that has stopped moving go to sleep so physics skips it, and sprite.sleeping tells you whether it is asleep right now. The world has its own world.allowSleeping switch too.\n\nsprite.applyTorque(t) gives it a push to spin, the way applyForce gives a push to move. sprite.applyForceScaled(fx, fy) is applyForce multiplied by the sprite's mass, so a heavy sprite and a light one speed up equally. sprite.resetMass() makes the sprite recalculate its mass. sprite.centerOfMass is where the weight balances, in pixels, and you can only read it."},{"title":"Motion and direction helpers","body":"sprite.setSpeedAndDirection(speed, direction) sets both at once: sprite.setSpeedAndDirection(5, 0) moves the sprite right at 5 pixels a frame. sprite.isMoving is true while the sprite is moving.\n\nsprite.angleToFace(x, y) tells you how many degrees the sprite would have to turn to face a point, the short way round: a positive number is a clockwise turn, or counter-clockwise in math mode. sprite.rotationToFace(x, y) gives the rotation the sprite would end up with instead. Both also take a sprite or anything with an x and a y.\n\nsprite.prevPos is where the sprite was at the end of the last frame and sprite.prevRotation is how it was turned, so you can see how far it moved this frame. sprite.scaleBy(x, y) multiplies the sprite's current scale, so scaleBy(2) doubles how big it is drawn. It changes only how it looks. The collider keeps its size."},{"title":"Sprite housekeeping: removed, groups, joints, screenSpace","body":"sprite.removed is true once a sprite has been deleted, which is useful if you kept a variable pointing at it. sprite.groups lists every group the sprite is in, and sprite.joints lists every joint attached to it.\n\nsprite.autoDraw = false stops the engine drawing the sprite, and sprite.autoUpdate = false stops it running the sprite's update. Use them when you want to draw or update that sprite yourself. For a whole group, see Groups: Drawing sprites yourself.\n\nsprite.screenSpace = true pins a sprite to the screen: it ignores the camera and is drawn on top of everything, which is what a score display or a health bar wants.\n\nsprite.textStroke is the outline color of a sprite's text, and sprite.textStrokeWeight is how thick that outline is."}]},{"slug":"physics","title":"Physics","pages":[{"title":"Gravity and velocity","body":"world.gravity.y pulls every dynamic sprite downward. It starts at 0, so nothing falls until you set it. 10 feels roughly earth-like; leave it at 0 for top-down games, or try negative values for \"upside-down\" worlds.\n\nEach sprite has a .vel vector with .x and .y components, measured in pixels per frame. At 60 fps, vel.x = 4 means the sprite moves 240 pixels per second. Reading .vel tells you how fast a sprite is moving; writing it overrides physics for that frame.\n\nAssigning vel every frame is fine for player controls — you're telling the engine exactly what you want.\n\nThere's a top speed. No sprite moves faster than world.maxSpeed pixels per frame, which starts at 60. Set vel.x higher and the sprite settles to 60 on the next frame, and the console says so once. That's a safety limit so fast sprites can't skip through things in a single frame. The next page shows how to raise it, and what it costs.","code":"let player, ground;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n  player = new Sprite(200, 100, 40, 40);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n  ground = new Sprite(200, 380, 400, 20, 'static');\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -5;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 5;\n  else                           player.vel.x = 0;\n\n  if (kb.presses(' ') && player.colliding(ground)) {\n    player.vel.y = -8;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"The speed cap: world.maxSpeed","body":"No sprite moves faster than world.maxSpeed pixels per frame. It starts at 60. Set vel.x = 150 and the sprite settles to 60 on the next frame, and the console says so once, naming world.maxSpeed.\n\nThe cap is there for a reason. A sprite that moves a long way in one frame can skip clean past a thin sprite between two frames without the two ever touching: a fast bullet can fly through an enemy. A moving solid (dynamic) sprite still stops at a static wall, because the physics engine checks that case between frames. A kinematic sprite, or one with collider = 'none', does not get that check.\n\nTo go faster, raise the cap in setup(), before you set the speed. Keep it as low as your game allows, and make thin targets a little thicker if a fast sprite misses them.","code":"let ball;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(600, 200);\n  world.maxSpeed = 120;   // pixels per frame; the default is 60\n  ball = new Sprite(30, 100, 20);\n  ball.vel.x = 100;       // over 60, allowed because of the line above\n  new Sprite(590, 100, 20, 200, 'static');\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Bounciness and friction","body":".bounciness (also called restitution) is how much speed a sprite keeps after a collision. 0 means it sticks, 1 means it bounces back at full speed, values above 1 add energy (fun, but unstable).\n\n.friction resists sliding along a surface. 0 is ice — sprites slide forever. Higher values drag motion to a stop quickly.\n\nThese are per-sprite properties. When two sprites touch, Box2D combines their values — bounciness uses the higher of the two, friction uses a geometric mean.","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n\n  const floor = new Sprite(200, 380, 400, 20, 'static');\n  floor.friction = 0.3;\n\n  const bouncy = new Sprite(120, 80, 30);\n  bouncy.bounciness = 0.9;\n  bouncy.color = 'tomato';\n\n  const dead = new Sprite(200, 80, 30);\n  dead.bounciness = 0;\n  dead.color = 'slategray';\n\n  const slippery = new Sprite(280, 80, 30);\n  slippery.friction = 0;\n  slippery.bounciness = 0.4;\n  slippery.color = 'deepskyblue';\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Walls around the canvas","body":"new Walls() puts four static walls just outside the edges of the canvas, so sprites bounce off the edges of the screen instead of falling out of it. The walls sit off screen, so you don't see them, and the whole canvas stays playable. Make them in setup(), after new Canvas().\n\nWhat you get back is a Group of the four walls, and each one also has a name: walls.top, walls.bottom, walls.left and walls.right. player.colliding(walls) asks \"is the player touching any edge?\" and player.colliding(walls.bottom) asks \"is it on the floor?\"\n\nnew Walls(100) makes them thicker (the default is 50), which helps if something fast keeps slipping through. The walls stay where the canvas was when you made them; they do not follow the camera.","code":"let walls;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n  walls = new Walls();\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {\n    const ball = new Sprite(40 + i * 35, 60 + (i % 3) * 40, 24);\n    ball.bounciness = 0.8;\n    ball.vel.x = i % 2 === 0 ? 6 : -6;\n    ball.color = i % 2 === 0 ? 'tomato' : 'gold';\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Mass and density","body":".mass is how heavy a sprite is — read it to see how physics will treat the sprite (a heavier sprite reacts less to the same applyForce push, see below). Setting .mass adjusts the sprite's density so its actual mass matches what you asked for. Static and kinematic sprites always have 0 mass, since gravity and pushes don't affect them.\n\n.density is the more direct knob underneath .mass — mass is density times the sprite's area, so a bigger sprite at the same density weighs more. New sprites default to a density of 1.\n\nYou'll reach for .mass far more often than .density — it's the number that actually means something (\"this rock should be twice as heavy as that crate\").","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n\n  new Sprite(200, 380, 400, 20, 'static');\n\n  const feather = new Sprite(120, 80, 30);\n  feather.color = 'lightyellow';\n  feather.mass = 0.2;\n\n  const rock = new Sprite(280, 80, 30);\n  rock.color = 'gray';\n  rock.mass = 5;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Drag (slowing down)","body":".drag slows a sprite down on its own, with no wall or floor to rub against. It is the knob for a ball on a table seen from above, where gravity is 0 and nothing else would ever stop it. 0 means no slowing at all.\n\nThe number is a rate per SECOND, not per frame. At .drag = 1 a moving sprite keeps about a third of its speed after one second and nearly none after four. At .drag = 0.02 it keeps almost all of it: the sprite crosses the canvas and hits a wall long before it slows. A few landmarks: 0.02 is nearly frictionless, 1 is a pool table, 6 is sand or thick carpet.\n\n.drag slows a sprite but never stops it exactly, because it only ever removes a share of what is left. To make a sprite really stop, check .speed each frame and set it to 0 once it is small enough.\n\n.friction is a different knob: it only acts while two sprites touch. .drag works everywhere, all the time.","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  const slick = new Sprite(40, 130, 24);\n  slick.color = 'lightblue';\n  slick.drag = 0.02;\n  slick.vel.x = 6;\n\n  const table = new Sprite(40, 200, 24);\n  table.color = 'lightgreen';\n  table.drag = 1;\n  table.vel.x = 6;\n\n  const sand = new Sprite(40, 270, 24);\n  sand.color = 'khaki';\n  sand.drag = 6;\n  sand.vel.x = 6;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Applying forces","body":"sprite.applyForce(fx, fy) pushes a sprite with a force in newtons — the physics engine's own force unit, not pixels. Pass the x and y components as two separate numbers. Call it every frame for continuous thrust, or once for a shove.\n\nForces respect mass — heavier sprites react less to the same push than light ones do. This is different from setting .vel directly, which overrides physics entirely.\n\napplyForce is good for rockets, wind, magnets, or any continuous push where you want the physics engine to handle the acceleration naturally.","code":"let rocket;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n  new Sprite(200, 380, 400, 20, 'static');\n\n  rocket = new Sprite(200, 300, 20, 60);\n  rocket.color = 'tomato';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('up'))    rocket.applyForce(0, -40);\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))  rocket.applyForce(-20, 0);\n  if (kb.pressing('right')) rocket.applyForce(20, 0);\n\n  if (kb.presses(' ')) rocket.applyForce(0, -200);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"World switches and explosions","body":"world.explodeAt(x, y, radius, magnitude, falloff) pushes every sprite near a point away from it. The push is strongest in the middle and fades to falloff times as strong at the edge of the radius. Physics never lets anything go faster than world.maxSpeed, so a very big magnitude makes every sprite in range fly off at the same top speed.\n\nworld.bounceThreshold is the slowest hit, in pixels per frame, that still makes a sprite bounce. Slower hits just stop.\n\nworld.allowSleeping lets resting sprites go to sleep so the physics skips them. By default the world steps itself every frame. world.autoStep = false hands that job to you, and then you call world.physicsUpdate() when you want it to move forward by one step. Most sketches never need that."}]},{"slug":"collisions","title":"Collisions & Overlaps","pages":[{"title":"colliding vs overlapping","body":"There are two ways sprites can interact. Colliding is physical contact — the sprites push each other and bounce. Overlapping is just bounding-box intersection — the sprites pass through each other but you can still detect the touch.\n\nDynamic and static sprites collide by default. To let one sprite pass through another without stopping, set its .collider to 'none' — the sprite still draws and you can still test for overlap, but there's no physical pushback.\n\nUse colliding for ground checks, walls, and enemies that block the player. Use overlapping for coins, goal zones, power-ups, and other triggers that shouldn't shove the player.\n\noverlaps() is a question you ask, not a setting you turn on. a.overlaps(b) answers \"are these touching right now?\" and changes nothing, so calling it once in setup() does not make a and b pass through each other (in p5play it does). To let a coin, a pickup or a bullet pass through, set its collider to 'none'; then ask overlaps() or overlapping() every frame in update() to find out when something reaches it.\n\nThe six questions follow one pattern. The -s word is the first frame, the -ing word counts frames while it lasts, and the -ed word is the frame it ends:\n\ncollides(b), colliding(b), collided(b) — the physical bodies touching.\noverlaps(b), overlapping(b), overlapped(b) — the shapes overlapping, whatever the colliders.\n\noverlaps() is the one exception: it is true on every frame the two overlap, not only the first. For the first frame only, ask overlapping(b) === 1.","code":"let player, wall, zone;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  player = new Sprite(80, 200, 30, 30);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n\n  wall = new Sprite(220, 200, 20, 200, 'static');\n  wall.color = 'slategray';\n\n  zone = new Sprite(340, 200, 60, 60);\n  zone.collider = 'none';\n  zone.color = 'gold';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -4;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 4;\n  else                           player.vel.x = 0;\n\n  if (player.overlapping(zone)) zone.color = 'lime';\n  else                          zone.color = 'gold';\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Overlap callbacks","body":"sprite.overlaps(other) returns true if the two sprites intersect (bounding-box math for rectangles, real circle-distance math for two circles). Pass a Group and it returns true if any member overlaps.\n\nYou can also pass a callback as the second argument: sprite.overlaps(group, (self, other) => { ... }). The callback fires once per overlapping pair, after the method has finished its own iteration — so it's safe to delete() sprites inside the callback.\n\nThis is the cleanest way to collect pickups: player.overlaps(coins, (p, coin) => { score++; coin.delete(); }).","code":"let player, coins;\nlet score = 0;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  player = new Sprite(200, 200, 30, 30);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n\n  coins = new Group();\n  coins.diameter = 18;\n  coins.color = 'gold';\n  coins.collider = 'none';\n  new coins.Sprite(120, 120);\n  new coins.Sprite(300, 260);\n  new coins.Sprite(260, 320);\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -4;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 4;\n  else                           player.vel.x = 0;\n  if (kb.pressing('up'))         player.vel.y = -4;\n  else if (kb.pressing('down'))  player.vel.y = 4;\n  else                           player.vel.y = 0;\n\n  player.overlaps(coins, (p, coin) => {\n    score++;\n    coin.delete();\n  });\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(16);\n  text('Score: ' + score, 10, 24);\n}"},{"title":"Collisions with groups","body":"The collision functions work against a single sprite or a whole Group. player.colliding(enemies) returns a truthy number (a running frame count) while the player touches any sprite in the enemies group.\n\ncolliding() reads real physics contacts from Box2D — it's not the same as the manual bounding-box math that overlaps() uses. Use colliding(ground) before a jump to confirm the player is standing on something solid.\n\nIf you need to know which sprite was hit, pass a callback, which works on all six questions: player.collides(enemies, (p, e) => e.delete()). Or iterate the group yourself and test one-by-one. When you delete sprites in response to a hit, loop over [...group] (a copy) so the shrinking group doesn't skip items.","code":"let player, enemies;\nlet hp = 3;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  player = new Sprite(60, 200, 30, 30);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n\n  enemies = new Group();\n  enemies.diameter = 24;\n  enemies.color = 'tomato';\n  enemies.collider = 'none';\n  new enemies.Sprite(220, 140);\n  new enemies.Sprite(260, 220);\n  new enemies.Sprite(320, 300);\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -3;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 3;\n  if (kb.pressing('up'))         player.vel.y = -3;\n  else if (kb.pressing('down'))  player.vel.y = 3;\n\n  for (const e of [...enemies]) {\n    if (player.overlapping(e)) {\n      hp--;\n      e.delete();\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(16);\n  text('HP: ' + hp, 14, 24);\n}"},{"title":"Ground sensors","body":"player.colliding(ground) is true whenever the player touches the ground anywhere: standing on it, but also pressed against it from the side or bumping it from below. If the floor, the walls and the ceiling are all one group, a jump check written with colliding() lets the player climb a wall by jumping against it.\n\nA sensor fixes that. sprite.addSensor(offsetX, offsetY, w, h) adds a box that rides on the sprite and only detects: it pushes nothing and weighs nothing. Put a thin one just under the player's feet, and feet.overlaps(walls) is true only when something is under them.\n\nThe offsets are measured from the sprite's centre. For a player 40 tall, the feet are 20 below the centre, so a strip 4 tall at offset 21 sits just under them. Make it a little narrower than the player so it doesn't touch a wall beside them.\n\nA sensor has three questions: feet.overlaps(walls) (true or false), feet.overlapping(walls) (how many frames it has been touching) and feet.overlapped(walls) (true on the frame it stops touching: the moment the player leaves the ground). Each takes a callback too, like a sprite's: feet.overlaps(walls, (sensor, wall) => { ... }). feet.remove() takes it off again; deleting the sprite does that too.\n\nAnother way to ask \"is something under me?\" is a ray: world.rayCast(player.x, player.y + 21, player.x, player.y + 27) looks along that short line and gives back what it hits first, or null if nothing is there. It looks straight through sensors and collider 'none' sprites, so a coin under your feet does not count as ground.","code":"let player, walls, feet;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n\n  walls = new Group();\n  walls.collider = 'static';\n  walls.color = 'slategray';\n  new walls.Sprite(200, 290, 400, 20);\n  new walls.Sprite(300, 200, 20, 160);\n  new walls.Sprite(120, 150, 120, 16);\n\n  player = new Sprite(100, 240, 30, 40);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n  player.rotationLock = true;\n  feet = player.addSensor(0, 21, 26, 4);\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -4;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 4;\n  else                           player.vel.x = 0;\n\n  if (kb.presses('up') && feet.overlaps(walls)) {\n    player.vel.y = -8;\n  }\n  player.color = feet.overlaps(walls) ? 'deepskyblue' : 'orange';\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Ray hits and sensors","body":"world.rayCast with no callback returns a hit or null. A hit has four things on it: hit.sprite is what the ray touched, hit.point is where, hit.normal is the direction the surface faces at that spot, and hit.fraction is how far along the ray it was, from 0 at the start to 1 at the end.\n\nA Sensor, from addSensor(), has a sprite property too: sensor.sprite is the sprite it rides on. So when a sensor answers true, sensor.sprite tells you whose it is."},{"title":"Ray casting","body":"world.rayCast(x1, y1, x2, y2, callback) casts a ray from one point to another and calls your callback for every physics shape the ray touches. The callback receives (sprite, point, normal, fraction): sprite is the Sprite that was hit (or null), point is the hit position in world pixels, normal is the surface normal (a unit vector pointing away from the surface), and fraction is how far along the ray the hit happened, from 0 to 1.\n\nWhat you return from the callback controls how the ray continues. Return the fraction you were handed (or a smaller number) to clip the ray there — the standard pattern for finding the single closest hit. Return 0 to stop at the first hit. Return 1 to keep going past every hit (all objects on the line). Return -1 to ignore a fixture — useful for skipping sensor sprites, which rayCast does report: filter them with if (sprite && sprite.collider === \"none\") return -1.\n\nRay casting is how you build hitscan weapons, line-of-sight checks, aim previews, and anchor-point picking: cast a ray toward a target and read where and what it first touches.","code":"let player, target, wall;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n\n  new Sprite(200, 290, 400, 20, \"static\");   // floor\n\n  player = new Sprite(50, 200, 20, 20, \"static\");\n  player.color = \"#5baafd\";\n\n  // A barrier with a gap under it: aim through the gap to reach the target,\n  // aim straight at the barrier and the ray stops here instead.\n  wall = new Sprite(200, 170, 20, 110, \"static\");\n  wall.color = \"#333844\";\n\n  target = new Sprite(320, 250, 30, 30);\n  target.color = \"tomato\";\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (mouse.presses()) {\n    let hit;\n    world.rayCast(player.x, player.y, mouse.x, mouse.y, function (sprite, point, normal, fraction) {\n      if (sprite && sprite.collider === \"none\") return -1; // skip sensors\n      hit = { sprite, point, normal, fraction };\n      return fraction; // clip at each hit to find the closest\n    });\n    if (hit && hit.sprite === target) {\n      hit.sprite.color = \"gold\";\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background(\"#222\");\n  // mouse.x/y read (0, 0) until the cursor has been over the canvas once.\n  if (!mouse.isActive) return;\n  stroke(\"#5baafd\");\n  strokeWeight(2);\n  line(player.x, player.y, mouse.x, mouse.y);\n}"}]},{"slug":"groups","title":"Groups","pages":[{"title":"Spawning and defaults","body":"A Group holds many sprites that share traits. Set a property on the group and every sprite spawned into it inherits that value as its default.\n\nSettings for how a sprite looks and what it is made of also change the sprites already in the group: enemies.color = 'red' turns every current enemy red, and the next one made too. That covers color, stroke, layer, visible, opacity, scale, text, size (w, h, diameter), collider, bounciness, friction, density and drag. A group's scale can change one direction on its own: walls.scale.y = 3 makes every wall three times as tall and leaves its width alone, and walls.scale = 2 sets both. Where a sprite is and how it moves (x, y, vel, rotation, life) only set the starting value for new sprites, and so does any property you invent yourself, like enemies.hp = 3.\n\nCreate a group with new Group(). Then spawn sprites into it with new groupName.Sprite(...) — note the capital S. That factory reads the group's color, diameter, collider, and other defaults, copies them onto the new sprite, and adds the sprite to the group.\n\nCommon defaults to set right after creating a group: color, diameter (for circles) or w/h, collider ('none' is useful for pickups and decorations), and layer.","code":"let stars;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  stars = new Group();\n  stars.color = 'gold';\n  stars.diameter = 16;\n  stars.collider = 'none';\n\n  new stars.Sprite(100, 100);\n  new stars.Sprite(200, 200);\n  new stars.Sprite(300, 300);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#112');\n}"},{"title":"Copying a sprite: clone()","body":"sprite.clone() makes a new sprite that looks and behaves like one you already set up: the same size and shape, colour and outline, picture, texture or animation, layer, rotation, and the same physics settings (collider, bounciness, friction, density, drag). It also joins every group the original is in, so a check like player.colliding(pipes) sees the copy too.\n\nThe copy is made in the same spot and starts still. Move it straight away, in the same function: two solid sprites on top of each other push apart on the next frame. It does not copy motion, life, joints, or properties you made up yourself, like hp or points. Set those on the copy.\n\nclone() is for \"one more like that one\". For many sprites that share settings from the start, a group's factory (new pipes.Sprite(...)) is still the tidier tool.","code":"let pipes, upper, lower;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  pipes = new Group();\n  upper = new pipes.Sprite(300, 60, 50, 120, 'kinematic');\n  upper.color = 'green';\n  upper.stroke = 'darkgreen';\n  upper.strokeWeight = 3;\n\n  lower = upper.clone();   // same size, colour, outline, collider, groups\n  lower.y = 340;           // move it before the next frame\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('skyblue');\n  text(pipes.length + ' pipes', 10, 20);\n}"},{"title":"Tile maps from text","body":"new Tiles(map, x, y, w, h, legend) builds a level out of rows of text. map is an array of strings, one per row. legend says which Group each character builds: { '#': walls, 'c': coins }. Any character the legend doesn't name, like a space or a dot, is an empty cell.\n\nEach tile is made with new group.Sprite(), so the group's defaults apply exactly as if you had made it by hand: walls.collider = 'static' makes every wall static, and coins.diameter = 14 makes every coin a small circle instead of a full tile.\n\nx and y are the centre of the top-left tile, the same way a sprite's x and y are its centre, and w and h are the size of one tile. For 40-pixel tiles starting in the canvas corner, pass 20, 20, 40, 40.\n\nWhat you get back is itself a Group holding every sprite it made, so level.delete() clears the whole level when you want to load the next one.","code":"let walls, coins, player, level;\nlet score = 0;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 240);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n\n  walls = new Group();\n  walls.collider = 'static';\n  walls.color = 'slategray';\n\n  coins = new Group();\n  coins.collider = 'none';\n  coins.diameter = 14;\n  coins.color = 'gold';\n\n  level = new Tiles([\n    '##########',\n    '#        #',\n    '#  c  c  #',\n    '#  ##  c #',\n    '# c      #',\n    '##########',\n  ], 20, 20, 40, 40, { '#': walls, 'c': coins });\n\n  player = new Sprite(60, 100, 20, 28);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n  player.rotationLock = true;\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -3;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 3;\n  else                           player.vel.x = 0;\n  if (kb.presses('up')) player.vel.y = -6;\n\n  player.overlaps(coins, (p, coin) => {\n    coin.delete();\n    score++;\n  });\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#112');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(14);\n  text('coins: ' + score, 50, 32);\n}"},{"title":"Iterating and removing","body":"A Group extends Array. You get .length, indexing with group[i], for..of loops, and array methods like .forEach directly on the group.\n\nOne trap: removing sprites while iterating shifts the array and skips items. Iterate over a copy when you might remove entries: for (const s of [...group]). The spread makes a snapshot so the original can shrink safely.\n\ngroup.remove(sprite) pulls the sprite out of the group but leaves it in the world — it still draws and runs physics. sprite.delete() destroys the sprite entirely and unparents it from every group. These are different operations with different effects.","code":"let drops;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  drops = new Group();\n  drops.color = 'deepskyblue';\n  drops.diameter = 14;\n  drops.collider = 'none';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (frameCount % 10 === 0) {\n    new drops.Sprite(Math.random() * 400, 0);\n  }\n  for (const d of [...drops]) {\n    d.y += 3;\n    if (d.y > 420) d.delete();\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#112');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(14);\n  text('drops: ' + drops.length, 14, 22);\n}"},{"title":"Culling sprites that leave the screen","body":"Every spawner has the same problem: sprites that drift off the edge are invisible but still alive, still costing physics every frame. Left alone, a game that fires a bullet every 10 frames is dragging thousands of dead bullets around after a few minutes.\n\ngroup.cull(margin) deletes the members that have gone that far outside the canvas, and returns how many it deleted. cull(0) means \"the moment they leave\", cull(50) gives them a 50-pixel grace border so a sprite that is only halfway off doesn't vanish mid-view.\n\nGive it a function and it runs that instead of deleting: cull(0, s => s.y = 0). That's how you wrap a sprite around to the other side, or send it back to the spawn point, rather than destroying it. The sprite stays in the group either way.\n\nIt measures against the camera, so a scrolling game culls against what's actually on screen, not against the origin.","code":"let drops;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  drops = new Group();\n  drops.color = 'deepskyblue';\n  drops.diameter = 14;\n  drops.collider = 'none';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (frameCount % 10 === 0) {\n    new drops.Sprite(Math.random() * 400, 0);\n  }\n  for (const d of drops) d.y += 3;\n\n  // one line replaces the remove-while-iterating loop\n  drops.cull(20);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#112');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(14);\n  text('alive: ' + drops.length, 14, 22);\n}"},{"title":"Sound","body":"loadSound('file.mp3') gives you a Sound. Call .play() for a one-off effect and .loop() for music that should keep going.\n\nThe built-in sounds load by name, no path needed: loadSound('coin'). They are blip, clang, coin, crown, dice, door, explode, hit, jump, laser, levelup, lose, music, pop, potion, powerup, select, step, sword, title, tone, whoosh and win. A sound you save under one of those names plays instead of the built-in one.\n\n.play() starts a fresh copy every time it's called, so firing a laser three times in three frames gives you three overlapping shots rather than one restarting over and over. .loop() is the opposite — it's the single track you start once and stop later, and calling it again while it's already going does nothing.\n\n.volume runs 0 to 1. .pan runs -1 (hard left) to 1 (hard right), so a sound can come from where the thing is: enemy.x < player.x ? -0.6 : 0.6. .rate is playback speed, and because it shifts the pitch too it's the cheapest way to get variety out of one sound: coin.rate = 1 + Math.random() * 0.3 makes every pickup sound slightly different. masterVolume(0.5) halves everything at once without touching any individual .volume — that's your mute button.\n\n.fade(to, seconds) ramps the volume instead of jumping it. music.fade(0, 2) is a two-second fade out, and it stops the sound when it lands so nothing keeps running silently.\n\nIf you have a lot of short effects, put them in one file and describe where each one lives: loadSound('sfx.mp3', { clips: { jump: [0, 0.25], hit: [0.5, 0.9] } }), then sfx.play('jump'). One download instead of a dozen. A clip name you haven't defined plays nothing, rather than blasting the whole file.\n\nTwo things that will confuse you the first time. Sound files have to download before they can play, so a .play() in the first moment of a sketch does nothing, and the console says so. Load them in preload() (\"Getting files ready\", Overview page 4) and they are ready by setup(). .loop() remembers: it starts as soon as the file arrives, so music.loop() in setup() works either way.\n\nAnd browsers keep sound off until the player has clicked or pressed a key, even for a sound that is ready. moSHion turns sound on at the first click or key press by itself. Before that, .play() does nothing and the console says \"sound turns on after the first click or key press\", so play effects on an event (a key, a hit), and start music with .loop(), which waits for that first click.","code":"let coin, music, player, coins;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  coin = loadSound('coin');\n  music = loadSound('music');\n  music.volume = 0.3;\n\n  player = new Sprite(60, 150, 24, 24);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n  player.collider = 'none';\n\n  coins = new Group();\n  coins.diameter = 14;\n  coins.color = 'gold';\n  coins.collider = 'none';\n  for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) new coins.Sprite(140 + i * 60, 150);\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  player.moveTowards(mouse, 0.08);\n\n  coins.overlaps(player, (c) => {\n    coin.rate = 1 + Math.random() * 0.3;   // a slightly different chime each time\n    coin.pan = (c.x - 200) / 200;          // and it comes from where the coin was\n    coin.play();\n    c.delete();\n  });\n\n  // safe to call every frame — loop() only ever starts one voice\n  music.loop();\n\n  // fade the music down as the coins run out\n  if (coins.length === 1 && music.volume > 0.1) music.fade(0.05, 1.5);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#111');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(13);\n  text('click to start the music, move to collect', 14, 24);\n}"},{"title":"The allSprites group","body":"allSprites is a built-in Group that every sprite joins automatically the moment it's created. You never construct it — it's already there as soon as moSHion loads.\n\nIt's useful whenever you want to touch \"every sprite in the scene\" at once: allSprites.length for a live count, or for (const s of allSprites) to iterate the whole world.\n\nSince it extends Array like any other Group, the same patterns apply.","code":"let player, rocks;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  player = new Sprite(200, 200, 20);\n  player.color = 'white';\n\n  rocks = new Group();\n  rocks.color = 'slategray';\n  rocks.diameter = 18;\n  rocks.collider = 'none';\n  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    new rocks.Sprite(60 + i * 60, 320);\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#112');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(14);\n  text('allSprites: ' + allSprites.length, 14, 22);\n}"},{"title":"Drawing sprites yourself: autoDraw and draw()","body":"The engine draws every sprite for you after draw() runs. To choose the moment yourself, turn that off with allSprites.autoDraw = false. Set it once, in setup(): it covers the sprites you make later too, like bullets made in the middle of the game.\n\nThen call allSprites.draw() where you want the sprites to appear, in update() or draw(). It draws them right then, lowest layer first, so you can paint the background, then the sprites, then words over the top. Any group works the same way: enemies.autoDraw = false and enemies.draw() for just the enemies.\n\nautoUpdate works like autoDraw: ghosts.autoUpdate = false stops the update you gave each ghost, for the ghosts you have now and the ones you make later. Set either one back to true to hand the job back to the engine.","code":"let player;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  allSprites.autoDraw = false; // I will draw the sprites myself\n  player = new Sprite(200, 150, 50, 50);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (mouse.presses()) {\n    let dot = new Sprite(mouse.x, mouse.y, 16);\n    dot.color = 'gold';\n    dot.collider = 'none';\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  allSprites.draw(); // the sprites go here\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(16);\n  text('Click to add a dot.', 20, 40); // drawn over every sprite\n}"},{"title":"Custom properties","body":"You aren't limited to moSHion's built-in properties. Assign any property you like on a group and it becomes a default for every sprite spawned afterward — enemies.hp = 3 gives every new enemy an hp of 3.\n\nEach sprite gets its own copy of the value on spawn, so changing one enemy's hp doesn't affect the others. The group just holds the template.\n\nThis is how you keep gameplay data attached to the right sprite. Tag sprites with custom fields like .hp, .score, .kind, or anything else your game needs, and read them back off the individual sprite during collisions.\n\nPick names the sprite doesn't already use. A sprite has built-in properties, and a field with the same name drives the built-in one instead of making a new field: speed sets its velocity (and reads back 0 on a static sprite), life deletes it after that many frames, direction turns it, and text draws a label on it. The same goes for color, scale, mass, x, y, w, h, d, r, width, height, visible and removed. Use names like hp, moveSpeed, heading or label. This matters most in a class that extends Sprite, where this.speed = 5 looks like your own field and isn't.","code":"let player, enemies;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  player = new Sprite(200, 200, 20);\n  player.color = 'white';\n\n  enemies = new Group();\n  enemies.color = 'tomato';\n  enemies.diameter = 20;\n  enemies.hp = 3;\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n    new enemies.Sprite(80 + i * 70, 80);\n  }\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (player.overlaps(enemies)) {\n    for (const e of enemies) {\n      if (player.overlaps(e)) { e.hp -= 1; if (e.hp <= 0) e.delete(); }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#112');\n}"},{"title":"More group tools: amount, contains, newSprite","body":"group.amount is how many sprites are in the group. Set it and the group makes new sprites or deletes extras until it matches: enemies.amount = 5. group.contains(s) is true if the sprite s is in the group. group.newSprite(x, y, w, h) makes a sprite in the group, the same as new group.Sprite(x, y, w, h). group.deleteAll() is the old name for group.delete().\n\nA sprite's s.groups list shows which groups that one sprite is in."},{"title":"Sound status: playing, loaded, duration","body":"A Sound you loaded can tell you how it is doing. sound.loaded is true once the file has downloaded and been decoded, and sound.duration is how long it is in seconds, which is 0 until then. sound.playing is true while it is making noise. sound.url is the name or address you loaded it with.\n\nIf something went wrong, sound.error holds a message and the sound stays silent. sound.blocked is true if the browser refused to start audio. That is rare, because the sketch unlocks sound by itself on the first click or key press."}]},{"slug":"camera","title":"Camera","pages":[{"title":"Following a target","body":"The camera controls which part of the world is visible. camera.x and camera.y are the viewport's center in world coordinates.\n\nThe simplest follow is to snap the camera to the player each frame: camera.x = player.x. It works, but the view jitters on every tiny movement.\n\nA soft follow eases the camera toward the player with a catch-up factor: camera.x += (player.x - camera.x) * 0.1. Smaller factors feel floaty; larger factors feel snappy. 0.1 is a good starting point.","code":"let player;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n  new Sprite(1000, 390, 2000, 20, 'static');\n  for (let x = 200; x < 2000; x += 300) {\n    new Sprite(x, 300, 60, 10, 'static');\n  }\n  player = new Sprite(200, 300, 30, 30);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -5;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 5;\n  if (kb.presses(' ')) player.vel.y = -8;\n\n  camera.x += (player.x - camera.x) * 0.1;\n  camera.y += (player.y - camera.y) * 0.1;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"camera.off() and camera.on()","body":"camera.off() makes everything you draw after it ignore the camera, so it stays put on the screen. camera.on() puts the camera back, and the world scrolls again. Use off() before drawing a score or a health bar and on() when you are done. A sprite with screenSpace = true does the same thing for itself."}]},{"slug":"input","title":"Input","pages":[{"title":"Keyboard basics","body":"The global kb object reports keyboard state every frame.\n\nkb.pressing('key') returns true while the key is held down. Use it for continuous actions like walking.\n\nkb.presses('key') is true only on the first frame of a press (edge-triggered). Use it for single-shot actions like jumping or firing.\n\nKey names are lowercase strings: 'left', 'right', 'up', 'down', 'a', 'w', 'enter', 'shift', 'escape', and either 'space' or ' ' for the space bar. Spelling is forgiving — 'space', 'ArrowRight' and 'arrowright' all find the key they name.\n\n'left', 'right', 'up' and 'down' answer for the arrow key OR its WASD twin, so kb.pressing('left') is true whether the player pressed the left arrow or 'a'. You get both control schemes without writing either one twice.\n\nTwo players on one keyboard need the opposite: W must move only one of them. Name the arrow itself and it answers for the arrow only: 'arrowUp', 'arrowDown', 'arrowLeft' and 'arrowRight' for one player, 'w', 'a', 's' and 'd' for the other.\n\nThere are shorthand properties too: kb.space, kb.enter, kb.shift, kb.arrowUp and friends give the same number kb.pressing() would.\n\nArrow keys and space bar are automatically prevented from scrolling the page.","code":"let player;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  player = new Sprite(200, 200, 40, 40);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -4;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 4;\n  else                           player.vel.x = 0;\n\n  if (kb.presses(' '))   player.color = 'gold';\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Holding a key","body":"kb.pressing('key') isn't just true/false — it's the number of frames the key has been held (0 when idle), so it's still truthy in an if-check but also useful as a charge-up timer.\n\nkb.holds('key') fires exactly once, the frame the hold crosses kb.holdThreshold (12 frames by default — about a fifth of a second at 60fps). kb.holding('key') is the frame count while held past that threshold (0 before it). kb.held('key') fires exactly once, the frame the key comes back up after having been held past the threshold — use it to tell a quick tap apart from a held press.\n\nmouse has the same four: mouse.holds(), mouse.holding(), mouse.held(), mouse.holdThreshold.","code":"let charge = 0;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 200);\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.holds(' ')) charge = 0;\n  if (kb.holding(' ')) charge = kb.holding(' ');\n  if (kb.held(' ')) console.log('released a full charge:', charge);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  fill('gold');\n  textSize(16);\n  text('hold space — charge: ' + charge, 14, 24);\n}"},{"title":"Mouse position and buttons","body":"mouse.x and mouse.y are the cursor's position in world coordinates (already camera-aware).\n\nmouse.pressing() returns true while any mouse button is held. mouse.presses() is true only on the first frame of a click — use it for single-shot actions. mouse.released() is true on the frame the button is released.\n\nTo tell the buttons apart, give the button's name: mouse.presses('right') is the first frame of a right-click, mouse.pressing('left') is true while the left button is held, and the same works for 'center' and for released(). With nothing in the brackets they answer for any button. Right-clicking the canvas does not open the browser's menu, so the right button is free for your game. mouse.left, mouse.center and mouse.right also count how many frames each button has been held. mouse.pos is the cursor as an {x, y} object, handy for passing straight to a method that takes a target.\n\nmouse.x and mouse.y are world coordinates — they already include the camera, so comparing them to a sprite's x and y always works. mouse.canvasPos is the same cursor measured in screen pixels instead. Use it for anything pinned to the screen rather than the world, like a HUD button: once the camera scrolls, mouse.x is somewhere out in the level while canvasPos is still where the cursor looks like it is.\n\nCombine mouse position with world.getSpriteAt(x, y) to build click-to-select or drag interactions.","code":"let dot;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  dot = new Sprite(200, 200, 24);\n  dot.color = 'white';\n  dot.collider = 'none';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  dot.x = mouse.x;\n  dot.y = mouse.y;\n\n  if (mouse.pressing())       dot.color = 'gold';\n  else                        dot.color = 'white';\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(14);\n  text('x: ' + Math.round(mouse.x) + '  y: ' + Math.round(mouse.y), 14, 22);\n}"},{"title":"Touch: a finger drives the mouse","body":"A single touch drives exactly the same mouse properties — mouse.x, mouse.y, mouse.presses(), mouse.pressing(), mouse.released(). A sketch written against the mouse works on a phone or tablet with no changes at all.\n\nThree things a tap does, all at once: it moves the cursor to where you touched, it starts a press (so mouse.presses() fires on that frame, reading the touch position), and it stops the page from scrolling or zooming out from under the canvas. Dragging a finger across the canvas keeps the press held and keeps mouse.x/y following the finger, like holding the left button down.\n\nMulti-touch is deliberately not mapped. A second finger changes nothing, and there is no touch gesture that stands in for the right mouse button — a sketch that needs two buttons needs a keyboard as well. mouse.isActive also turns true on the first touch, exactly as it does on the first mouse move.","code":"let dot;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  dot = new Sprite(200, 200, 40);\n  dot.color = 'white';\n  dot.collider = 'none';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  dot.x = mouse.x;\n  dot.y = mouse.y;\n  dot.color = mouse.pressing() ? 'gold' : 'white';\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}\n\nfunction drawTop() {\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(14);\n  text('tap or drag anywhere', 14, 22);\n}"},{"title":"Hit-testing with the mouse","body":"world.getSpriteAt(x, y) returns the top-most sprite at that world position, or undefined if nothing is there: the highest layer, and between two on the same layer, the one made later (the one drawn on top). It checks both circles (radius test) and rectangles (bounding box). It also takes a point, world.getSpriteAt(mouse), and it finds a screenSpace HUD sprite where it is drawn, even after the camera has scrolled.\n\nworld.getSpritesAt(x, y) returns every sprite there, top-most first.\n\nThis is the pattern for click-to-select: on mouse.presses(), call world.getSpriteAt(mouse.x, mouse.y) and remember the result. Then you can drag it, change its color, or delete it.","code":"let selected;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) {\n    let s = new Sprite(60 + i * 55, 200, 40, 40);\n    s.color = 'steelblue';\n  }\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (mouse.presses()) {\n    if (selected) selected.color = 'steelblue';\n    selected = world.getSpriteAt(mouse.x, mouse.y);\n    if (selected) selected.color = 'tomato';\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Every named key: kb.arrowLeft, kb.ctrl and the rest","body":"For the common keys the kb object has a true or false property that is true while the key is held: kb.space, kb.enter, kb.escape, kb.tab, kb.backspace, kb.shift, kb.control (or kb.ctrl), kb.alt (or kb.opt, or kb.option), kb.capsLock, kb.arrowUp, kb.arrowDown, kb.arrowLeft, kb.arrowRight, and kb.meta (or kb.cmd, or kb.command, or kb.win, or kb.windows) for the key with the logo on it. kb.arrowLeft is the same as kb.pressing('arrowLeft').\n\nkb.pressed(key) and kb.releases(key) are old names that are easy to mix up: kb.pressed fires when the key comes back UP, the same as released(). To catch the moment a key goes down, use presses()."},{"title":"The mouse and a sprite: hover, click, hide the pointer","body":"Every sprite has a .mouse that answers questions about the mouse and that one sprite.\n\nbutton.mouse.hovering() is true while the pointer is over the sprite. button.mouse.hovers() is true only on the first frame it gets there, and button.mouse.hovered() only on the first frame after it leaves: handy for a sound or a grow effect that should happen once.\n\nbutton.mouse.presses() is true on the frame a click starts on the sprite. button.mouse.pressing() stays true while the button is held down, even if the pointer slides off the sprite, which is just what dragging needs. button.mouse.released() is true on the frame you let go. Like mouse.presses(), each can take 'left', 'center' or 'right'.\n\nTo hide the pointer, for a game with its own crosshair sprite, write mouse.visible = false. mouse.visible = true brings it back. mouse.pressing() (no sprite) is true while any mouse button is down anywhere.","code":"let button, crosshair;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  mouse.visible = false; // the crosshair sprite is the pointer now\n\n  button = new Sprite(200, 150, 160, 60, 'static');\n  button.color = 'slateblue';\n  button.text = 'Click me';\n\n  crosshair = new Sprite(0, 0, 12);\n  crosshair.collider = 'none';\n  crosshair.color = 'white';\n  crosshair.layer = 10;\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  crosshair.x = mouse.x;\n  crosshair.y = mouse.y;\n\n  if (button.mouse.hovering()) button.color = 'mediumslateblue';\n  else button.color = 'slateblue';\n\n  if (button.mouse.pressing()) button.scale = 0.9;\n  else button.scale = 1;\n\n  if (button.mouse.presses()) button.text = 'Clicked!';\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"More about the mouse: drags, scroll, cursor","body":"mouse.position is another name for mouse.pos. mouse.isOnCanvas is true while the pointer is over the canvas.\n\nmouse.drags() is true on the first frame of a drag, mouse.dragging() counts the frames the pointer has been moving with a button held, and mouse.dragged() is true on the frame the drag ends. Each takes a button name: 'left', 'center' or 'right'.\n\nmouse.scrollDelta has an x and a y for how far the wheel moved since the last frame, and is 0 when it did not move. mouse.cursor changes the shape of the pointer over the canvas, with a CSS cursor name: mouse.cursor = 'pointer' gives a pointing hand, and mouse.cursor = 'none' hides it. mouse.visible = false hides the pointer too, and mouse.visible = true brings back the one you had before.\n\nmouse.pressed(button) and mouse.releases(button) are old names, like the kb ones: use presses() and released()."}]},{"slug":"joints","title":"Joints","pages":[{"title":"GlueJoint","body":"A GlueJoint welds two sprites together so they move and rotate as a single rigid body. Create it with new GlueJoint(spriteA, spriteB) — just the two sprites, no options object.\n\nOnce glued, changing one sprite's position or velocity carries the other along. Gluing a dynamic sprite to a static one effectively pins it in place.\n\nLike every joint, call joint.delete() to break the connection and let the sprites move independently again.","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n  new Sprite(200, 380, 400, 20, 'static');\n\n  const stem = new Sprite(200, 200, 20, 100);\n  const cap = new Sprite(200, 150, 100, 20);\n  new GlueJoint(stem, cap);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"DistanceJoint","body":"A DistanceJoint keeps two sprites at a fixed distance — the basis for ropes, tethers, and pendulum strings. Create it with new DistanceJoint(spriteA, spriteB, options).\n\nSet joint.length after construction to change the target distance. The length is in pixels. You can also pass { length: 100 } in the options object at construction time.\n\nCall joint.delete() to remove the constraint.","code":"let tether;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n\n  const anchor = new Sprite(200, 60, 16, 16, 'static');\n  const ball = new Sprite(320, 60, 30);\n  ball.color = 'tomato';\n\n  tether = new DistanceJoint(anchor, ball);\n  tether.length = 120;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"HingeJoint","body":"A HingeJoint pins two sprites at a shared point and lets them rotate around it. Use it for pendulums, swinging doors, and anything that needs to pivot.\n\nCreate it with new HingeJoint(spriteA, spriteB, options). Pass { anchor: { x, y } } in the options to set the pivot point in world coordinates — otherwise it defaults to spriteA's position.\n\nCall joint.delete() to remove the hinge.","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n\n  const anchor = new Sprite(200, 80, 16, 16, 'static');\n  const rod = new Sprite(200, 180, 16, 180);\n  rod.color = 'khaki';\n\n  new HingeJoint(anchor, rod);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Stiff hinges and limits","body":"A plain hinge swings freely, so a body built from hinges (a ragdoll, a robot arm) goes floppy. Two settings fix that.\n\nstiffness, from 0 to 1, is how hard the hinge tries to stay in the pose it started in. 0 (the default) is floppy. 1 holds a limb up against gravity and springs it back after a hit. Something in between sags a little.\n\nlowerLimit and upperLimit stop the hinge at an angle, in degrees, counted from the starting pose in the same direction as rotation. An elbow that only bends one way is { lowerLimit: 0, upperLimit: 140 }. Set one to null to take that limit off.\n\nPass them in the options, new HingeJoint(a, b, { stiffness: 0.6, lowerLimit: -45, upperLimit: 45 }), or change them later: elbow.stiffness = 0. joint.angle tells you how far it has turned from the starting pose.\n\nPut the anchor where the two parts really meet (a shoulder, a knee). Without one, the pivot is the middle of the first sprite.","code":"let elbow;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n\n  const shoulder = new Sprite(120, 100, 16, 16, 'static');\n  const upper = new Sprite(170, 100, 100, 18);\n  const lower = new Sprite(260, 100, 80, 14);\n  upper.color = 'khaki';\n  lower.color = 'orange';\n\n  new HingeJoint(shoulder, upper, { anchor: { x: 120, y: 100 }, stiffness: 1 });\n  elbow = new HingeJoint(upper, lower, {\n    anchor: { x: 220, y: 100 },\n    stiffness: 0.5,\n    lowerLimit: 0,\n    upperLimit: 120,\n  });\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  // Hold the mouse down to make the elbow floppy.\n  elbow.stiffness = mouse.pressing() ? 0 : 0.5;\n  text('elbow: ' + round(elbow.angle) + ' degrees', 10, 20);\n}"},{"title":"SliderJoint","body":"A SliderJoint locks two sprites to a single axis of motion and forbids rotation between them. It's perfect for pistons, elevators, and sliding panels.\n\nCreate it with new SliderJoint(spriteA, spriteB, options). Pass { axis: { x, y } } to set the slide direction — { x: 0, y: 1 } for vertical, { x: 1, y: 0 } for horizontal. Drive the moving sprite with .vel along the axis.\n\nCall joint.delete() to remove the constraint.","code":"let base, piston;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  base = new Sprite(200, 200, 20, 20, 'static');\n  piston = new Sprite(200, 260, 80, 20);\n  piston.color = 'orange';\n\n  new SliderJoint(base, piston, { axis: { x: 0, y: 1 } });\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('up'))        piston.vel.y = -3;\n  else if (kb.pressing('down')) piston.vel.y = 3;\n  else                          piston.vel.y = 0;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"WheelJoint","body":"WheelJoint is built for vehicles. Pass the chassis as spriteA and the wheel as spriteB. The wheel can travel along an axis (giving you suspension) and spin freely.\n\nCreate it with new WheelJoint(chassis, wheel, options). Pass { axis: { x, y } } to set the suspension axis — { x: 0, y: 1 } for vertical suspension.\n\nDrive the wheel by setting its angularVelocity each frame. Call joint.delete() to remove the constraint.","code":"let chassis, wheel;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n  new Sprite(200, 380, 400, 20, 'static');\n\n  chassis = new Sprite(200, 300, 100, 20);\n  wheel = new Sprite(180, 330, 26);\n  wheel.friction = 1;\n  new WheelJoint(chassis, wheel, { axis: { x: 0, y: 1 } });\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('right'))     wheel.angularVelocity = 6;\n  else if (kb.pressing('left')) wheel.angularVelocity = -6;\n  else                          wheel.angularVelocity = 0;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"GrabberJoint","body":"A GrabberJoint pulls a physics sprite toward a target point with a spring force. It's how you drag boxes around in a physics puzzle: the box swings and tumbles on the way instead of teleporting.\n\nCreate it with new GrabberJoint(anchorSprite, draggedSprite, options). The anchor is any static sprite; the pull starts at the dragged sprite. Pass { maxForce: 200 } to control how strong the pull is — higher values feel snappier.\n\nMove the target by setting joint.target to a point. grab.target = mouse every frame drags the sprite after the cursor.\n\nCall joint.delete() (or joint.remove()) to let go.","code":"let box, anchor, grab;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n  new Sprite(200, 380, 400, 20, 'static');\n\n  anchor = new Sprite(0, 0, 4, 4, 'static');\n  anchor.visible = false;\n\n  box = new Sprite(200, 300, 50, 50);\n  box.color = 'deepskyblue';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (mouse.presses() && world.getSpriteAt(mouse) === box) {\n    grab = new GrabberJoint(anchor, box, { maxForce: 200 });\n  }\n  if (grab) grab.target = mouse;\n  if (mouse.released() && grab) {\n    grab.delete();\n    grab = null;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Joint: what every joint shares","body":"Every joint (GlueJoint, DistanceJoint, HingeJoint, SliderJoint, WheelJoint, GrabberJoint) is a Joint. Make one with two different sprites that are both still in the game. If either is not a sprite, or you give the same one twice, you get a plain error.\n\njoint.delete() takes a joint away, and joint.remove() does the same. sprite.joints lists the joints on a sprite.\n\nJoints take an options object as the last argument. anchor is the point in the world where the joint pins the two sprites together, and each kind of joint has its own default for it. lowerLimit and upperLimit belong to HingeJoint: they stop it turning past an angle, in degrees from the pose it started in. maxForce is how hard a GrabberJoint is allowed to pull, and it is 100 unless you say otherwise."}]},{"slug":"animation","title":"Animation","pages":[{"title":"Procedural animation","body":"Not every animation needs a sprite sheet. The global frameCount increments every frame — combining it with Math.sin, Math.cos, and the modulo operator is enough for bobbing, spinning, pulsing, and color cycling.\n\nMath.sin(frameCount / period) oscillates between -1 and 1. Multiply by an amplitude and add a baseline to shift it into the range you want.\n\nModulo (frameCount % n) cycles through integers 0..n-1 each n frames — great for flashing effects or phase changes.","code":"let orb;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  orb = new Sprite(200, 200, 50);\n  orb.collider = 'none';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  orb.x = 200 + 120 * Math.cos(frameCount / 40);\n  orb.y = 200 + 60 * Math.sin(frameCount / 20);\n  orb.rotation = frameCount * 3;\n  orb.scale.x = 1 + 0.25 * Math.sin(frameCount / 15);\n  orb.scale.y = orb.scale.x;\n  orb.color = (frameCount % 60 < 30) ? 'cyan' : 'magenta';\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"addAni and changeAni","body":"moSHion supports minimal sprite-sheet animation. sprite.addAni(name, sheetUrl, frameCount) loads a horizontal frame strip from an image URL and registers it under the given name. The first addAni call auto-activates that animation.\n\nsprite.changeAni(name) switches to a previously registered animation. If the name isn't found, nothing changes, and the console says so once, listing the names the sprite does have — a misspelt name otherwise looks just like a frozen sprite.\n\nframeDelay controls how many game frames each animation frame is held for (default 4). Set it on the animation addAni returns, or on sprite.texture.frameDelay: after addAni, sprite.texture is the animation showing, and sprite.texture = 'fly' switches to the sprite's own animation called 'fly', the same as changeAni('fly'). (sprite.ani is an older name for the same thing and still works.)\n\nloop is true by default, so an animation repeats forever. Set sprite.texture.loop = false to play it once and stop on the last frame, like a door that opens and stays open. Set sprite.texture.loop to a number to play it that many times, so sprite.texture.loop = 3 plays three times and then stays on the last frame. Set sprite.texture.frame = 0 to play it again (the count starts over). To freeze an animation on the picture showing, call sprite.texture.pause(), and sprite.texture.play() carries on from there. sprite.texture.paused is true while it is frozen.\n\nsprite.texture.frame is the frame showing, starting at 0. Set it to jump: sprite.texture.frame = 2 shows the third frame from the start of its time on screen, and -1 is the last frame. sprite.texture.tick is how many game frames the current frame has been showing; set it after frame to start part-way through a frame that lasts more than one (sprite.texture.frame = 1; sprite.texture.tick = 1; is the second game frame of frame 1).\n\nSetting sprite.image clears any active animation, and vice versa — they're mutually exclusive.","code":"let ghost;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n\n  ghost = new Sprite(200, 200, 80, 80);\n  ghost.collider = 'none';\n\n  // Two frame strips that ship with moSHion.\n  // 'idle' is the first addAni, so it starts playing straight away.\n  ghost.addAni('idle', 'assets/ghost_idle.avif', 7);\n  const fly = ghost.addAni('fly', 'assets/ghost_fly.avif', 4);\n  fly.frameDelay = 3; // flap a little faster than the default 4\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  // hold the right arrow (or D) to fly; let go to hover\n  if (kb.pressing('right')) {\n    ghost.x += 2;\n    if (ghost.x > 440) ghost.x = -40; // wrap back to the left edge\n    ghost.changeAni('fly');\n  } else {\n    ghost.changeAni('idle');\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Animation objects: Ani, StillTexture and Anis","body":"sprite.texture gives you back an object. For a texture that moves it is an Ani, and for a single picture it is a StillTexture. Both have a name, a frame, a frameCount, frameDelay and loop, so you can read them the same way. A still has frameCount 1 and nothing for it to animate.\n\nsprite.texture.frameDelays is an optional list with one number per frame, saying how many game frames to hold that frame for. It is null when every frame lasts frameDelay frames.\n\nsprite.texture.spriteSheet is the loaded picture the frames are cut from. Printing a texture shows its name (that is its toString), so text('using ' + sprite.texture) reads using coin. Anis is the type that holds Ani objects by name."}]},{"slug":"text","title":"Text & HUD","pages":[{"title":"Displaying text","body":"text(string, x, y) draws text on the canvas at (x, y). Call it from draw() so it gets re-drawn every frame — otherwise background() will erase it.\n\ntextSize(px) sets the font size in pixels. fill(color) sets the text color (yes, the same fill you use for shapes). textAlign('left' | 'center' | 'right') changes how x positions the string — 'left' puts x at the left edge, 'center' puts x at the middle.\n\nYou can also pass size and color directly to text(): text('hello', 10, 20, 18, 'gold'). The constants CENTER, LEFT, and RIGHT are available for textAlign.\n\nFor a different typeface, bold, italic, underlines, outlines and wrapped lines, see the next three pages: Fonts, Weight and style, and Lines, outlines and spacing.\n\nYou can build strings with + just like normal JavaScript: 'Score: ' + score.","code":"let score = 0;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.presses(' ')) score++;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#112');\n\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(28);\n  textAlign(CENTER);\n  text('moSHion', 200, 80);\n\n  fill('gold');\n  textSize(18);\n  textAlign(LEFT);\n  text('Score: ' + score, 20, 140);\n\n  fill('#888');\n  textSize(12);\n  text('press space to score', 20, 160);\n}"},{"title":"Fonts: textFont()","body":"textFont('name') picks the typeface for the text() calls that follow. Like textSize(), it lasts until you change it.\n\nThe built-in fonts:\n'sans' clean and plain\n'serif' like a book\n'code' code, every letter the same width\n'rounded' soft round ends\n'pixel' pixel art\n'arcade' old arcade screens\n'handwriting' written with a marker\n'comic' comic book\n'bubbly' round and bubbly\n'space' sci-fi\n'spooky' dripping horror\n'slab' thick square feet\n'blocky' big blocky signs\n'script' joined-up handwriting\n'tall' tall and narrow\n\ntextFont('monospace') goes back to the default font.\n\nA name it does not know prints \"No font named ...\" in the console once, and the text keeps the font it had. Check the spelling: font names are in quotes and are case-sensitive.\n\nFonts load while your sketch starts. The preview waits a moment for the fonts named in textFont('...'), so the first frame is right. A name built up from pieces (textFont('pix' + 'el')) works too, but can show the default font for a moment first.","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#1b1d2a');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(26);\n\n  textFont('arcade');\n  text('GAME OVER', 20, 50);\n\n  textFont('handwriting');\n  text('Nice try!', 20, 110);\n\n  textFont('spooky');\n  fill('limegreen');\n  text('The swamp wins', 20, 170);\n\n  textFont('rounded');\n  fill('gold');\n  text('Score: 120', 20, 230);\n}"},{"title":"Weight and style: textWeight(), textStyle()","body":"textWeight(n) sets how thick the letters are, from 100 (thin) to 900 (heavy). 400 is normal and 700 is bold. A font that does not have the weight you ask for uses its nearest one: 'sans' goes all the way from 100 to 900, but 'arcade' only has one weight.\n\ntextStyle(BOLD) is weight 700, textStyle(ITALIC) slants the letters, textStyle(BOLDITALIC) does both, and textStyle(NORMAL) goes back to plain, upright, weight 400. Each textStyle() call replaces the last one, so textStyle(ITALIC) followed by textStyle(BOLD) is just bold. For both, use BOLDITALIC.\n\nTo get slanted AND a particular weight, call textStyle(ITALIC) first, then textWeight(): textWeight() only changes the weight.","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#1b1d2a');\n  fill('white');\n  textFont('sans');\n  textSize(22);\n\n  textStyle(NORMAL);\n  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    let w = 100 + i * 200;\n    textWeight(w);\n    text('weight ' + w, 20, 40 + i * 34);\n  }\n\n  textStyle(ITALIC);\n  textWeight(800);\n  fill('orange');\n  text('slanted and heavy', 20, 250);\n}"},{"title":"Lines, outlines and spacing","body":"A '\\n' inside the words starts a new line: text('Line one\\nLine two', 20, 40).\n\ntextWrap(width) breaks long text onto new lines so it fits inside width pixels, between words. textWrap(width, CHAR) may break in the middle of a word, which suits a name with no spaces. textWrap() turns wrapping off.\n\ntextLeading(px) is the distance from one line to the next. textLeading(null) goes back to the normal gap (1.25 times the text size).\n\ntextDecoration(UNDERLINE) draws a line under the text, textDecoration(STRIKETHROUGH) a line through it, textDecoration(UNDERLINE, STRIKETHROUGH) both, and textDecoration(NONE) neither. The line is the text's colour.\n\ntextOutline('black', 3) draws a 3-pixel outline around every letter, which makes words easy to read on a busy background. textOutline() turns it off.\n\ntextSpacing(px) puts extra pixels between the letters (a negative number squeezes them together). textSpacing(0) is normal.\n\nAll of these last until you change them, like textSize(), and they work with textAlign(), textLayer() and every font.","code":"function setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 320);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#3a6');\n  textFont('bubbly');\n  textSize(30);\n  fill('white');\n  textOutline('black', 4);\n  textAlign(CENTER);\n  text('LEVEL 2', 200, 50);\n  textOutline();\n\n  textAlign(LEFT);\n  textFont('sans');\n  textSize(16);\n  fill('#112');\n  textWrap(220);\n  textLeading(22);\n  text('Collect every coin before the timer runs out. Watch out for the bats!', 20, 100);\n  textWrap();\n  textLeading(null);\n\n  textDecoration(UNDERLINE);\n  text('Press SPACE to start', 20, 220);\n  textDecoration(NONE);\n\n  textSpacing(6);\n  text('S P A C E D', 20, 270);\n  textSpacing(0);\n}"},{"title":"HUDs that don't scroll","body":"When you move camera.x to follow the player, everything drawn inside draw() scrolls with the world — including your score text. And there is a second, quieter problem: the engine draws every sprite AFTER draw(), so anything draw() paints lands underneath the sprites — a score in the top-left corner disappears behind whatever wall happens to be up there.\n\ndrawTop() is the answer to both. It runs after the sprites are on the canvas, with the camera off for its duration, so text('Score: ' + score, 10, 24) means 10 pixels from the canvas edge and lands on top of the world. Define it like any other hook — the engine calls it every frame if it exists, and a sketch without it runs exactly as before.\n\ncamera.off() around drawing inside draw() still works for positioning, and it is still the right tool when you want something under the sprites on purpose (a vignette, a floor grid). But a HUD wants drawTop().","code":"let player;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n  new Sprite(1000, 390, 2000, 20, 'static');\n  player = new Sprite(200, 300, 30, 30);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -5;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 5;\n  camera.x += (player.x - camera.x) * 0.1;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}\n\nfunction drawTop() {\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(16);\n  text('x: ' + Math.round(player.x), 10, 24);\n}"},{"title":"Text between sprites: textLayer()","body":"Text drawn in draw() goes under every sprite, and drawTop() puts it over all of them. To put words in between (a sign over the player but behind a tree), give the text a layer, the same way you give a sprite one.\n\ntextLayer(5) works like textSize(): every text() you write after it, in update() or draw(), goes on layer 5. It is painted over every sprite whose layer is 5 or lower, and under every sprite whose layer is higher. Change the number to move the text up or down. textLayer() with nothing in the brackets puts text back under every sprite.\n\nIt lasts until you change it, so text you write before textLayer() in the very first frame is the only text it misses. Text is not a sprite, so text.layer = 10 does nothing: write textLayer(10) instead. drawTop() is still the place for a score or a timer, because it goes over everything, whatever the layer.","code":"let player, tree;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  player = new Sprite(80, 150, 40, 40);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n  player.layer = 1;\n\n  tree = new Sprite(260, 150, 70, 120);\n  tree.collider = 'none';\n  tree.color = 'forestgreen';\n  tree.layer = 10;\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  player.vel.x = 0;\n  if (kb.pressing('left')) player.vel.x = -3;\n  if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 3;\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(24);\n  textLayer(5); // over the player (layer 1), under the tree (layer 10)\n  text('Welcome to the forest', 60, 160);\n  textLayer();  // back to under every sprite\n}"},{"title":"Labels on sprites","body":"sprite.text puts a label directly on a sprite — a name tag, an HP counter, a damage number — without you tracking its x/y yourself. It's centered over the sprite and follows it automatically as the sprite moves, and it stays right-side-up even if the sprite rotates. Set it to null (the default) to remove the label.\n\nsprite.textColor and sprite.textSize style it, same idea as fill()/textSize() for the global text() function but scoped to just this one sprite. It respects sprite.opacity too, so a fading sprite's label fades with it.\n\nThis is a different tool from the global text() function above: text() is for one-off draws you position yourself (titles, HUDs); sprite.text is for a label that's tied to a specific sprite.","code":"let enemy;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n\n  enemy = new Sprite(200, 200, 50, 50);\n  enemy.color = 'firebrick';\n  enemy.text = 'HP 3';\n  enemy.textColor = 'white';\n  enemy.textSize = 14;\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.presses(' ')) enemy.text = 'HP 2';\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Wrapping words: WORD and CHAR","body":"The second thing you give textWrap says where a line may break. WORD, the default, breaks only between words. CHAR may break in the middle of a word, which suits a name with no spaces. textWrap(220, WORD) is the same as textWrap(220)."}]},{"slug":"persistence","title":"Persistence","pages":[{"title":"Saving and loading data","body":"moSHion provides three functions for storing data in the browser's localStorage.\n\nstoreItem(name, value) saves a value under a key. The value is automatically JSON-stringified, so you can store numbers, strings, arrays, and objects.\n\ngetItem(name) retrieves a previously stored value, parsing it back from JSON. Returns null if the key doesn't exist.\n\nremoveItem(name) deletes a stored value.\n\nThis is how you save high scores, level progress, or player preferences between sessions. Data persists even after the browser is closed.","code":"let highScore = 0;\nlet score = 0;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  highScore = getItem('highScore') || 0;\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.presses(' ')) {\n    score++;\n    if (score > highScore) {\n      highScore = score;\n      storeItem('highScore', highScore);\n    }\n  }\n  if (kb.presses('r')) {\n    score = 0;\n    removeItem('highScore');\n    highScore = 0;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(18);\n  text('Score: ' + score, 14, 30);\n  fill('gold');\n  text('High: ' + highScore, 14, 54);\n  fill('#888');\n  textSize(12);\n  text('space=score  r=reset', 14, 76);\n}"},{"title":"Erasing and loading files: clearStorage and loadJSON","body":"clearStorage() forgets everything your sketch stored with storeItem. After it, getItem on any name gives null. It clears the whole store at once, not just one name, so for a Reset button on a single high score use removeItem('highScore') instead.\n\nloadJSON(path, callback) reads a JSON file and hands the data to your callback when it arrives: loadJSON('level1.json', (data) => { level = data; }). It also returns an object that fills in once the file has loaded. Outside preload() the callback form is the one to rely on, because a file takes a moment to arrive. Inside preload() (\"Getting files ready\", Overview page 4) the game waits for the file, so levels = loadJSON('levels.json') in preload() is filled in by setup(). A file that cannot load says so in the console."}]},{"slug":"patterns","title":"Game Patterns","pages":[{"title":"Top-down movement","body":"For top-down games, keep world.gravity.y at 0 (where it starts) so nothing falls. Writing world.gravity.y = 0 in setup() anyway makes that choice plain to a reader. Move the player by writing to vel.x and vel.y based on the arrow keys.\n\nClamp the player's position to the canvas edges each frame so it can't leave the screen. A simple Math.max / Math.min pair handles this.\n\nSetting vel to 0 when no key is pressed gives a snappy, arcade feel. For drifty movement, leave vel alone and add small amounts instead.","code":"let player;\nconst SPEED = 4;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  player = new Sprite(200, 200, 30, 30);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  player.vel.x = 0;\n  player.vel.y = 0;\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))  player.vel.x = -SPEED;\n  if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = SPEED;\n  if (kb.pressing('up'))    player.vel.y = -SPEED;\n  if (kb.pressing('down'))  player.vel.y = SPEED;\n\n  player.x = Math.max(15, Math.min(385, player.x));\n  player.y = Math.max(15, Math.min(385, player.y));\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Platformer jump","body":"A platformer needs gravity, a ground-check, and edge-triggered jump input. Keep the ground sprite in a variable so you can test player.colliding(ground) before jumping.\n\nUse kb.pressing for continuous left/right motion, and kb.presses(' ') for the jump so one tap equals one jump.\n\nFor a double jump, or a jump that a wall cannot hand back, set player.maxJumps = 2 and call player.jump(ground): the next page.","code":"let player, ground;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n  player = new Sprite(200, 100, 30, 40);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n  ground = new Sprite(200, 380, 400, 20, 'static');\n  new Sprite(120, 300, 80, 12, 'static');\n  new Sprite(300, 240, 80, 12, 'static');\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -5;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 5;\n\n  if (kb.presses(' ') && player.colliding(ground)) {\n    player.vel.y = -9;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Double jumps","body":"A sprite can count its own jumps. Set player.maxJumps = 2 and it may jump twice before it has to land again: 1 (the default) is an ordinary jump, 3 a triple jump. Set it in setup(), after the sprite has its size.\n\nplayer.jump(ground) in update() does the jump, but only while one is left. It gives back true when it jumped and false when it did not. ground is what the player jumps off, the same way player.colliding(ground) names it: a sprite, or a group such as platforms. Landing on it gives every jump back; landing on anything else (a crate, an enemy's head) does not. player.jump() with nothing in the brackets counts anything solid as ground. The player does not have to be touching ground to jump: with maxJumps 2 the second jump is in mid-air.\n\nplayer.jumpsLeft is how many jumps are left, and player.onGround is true while ground is under the sprite's feet. A wall beside it does not count, and neither does a coin or anything else with collider 'none'. Walking off a ledge uses up the first jump, so with maxJumps 1 there is no jumping in mid-air.\n\nplayer.jumpPower is how high it jumps. 1 (the default) is a normal jump of about 120 pixels, 2 is twice as high, and 0.5 is half as high. It is the same height whatever world.gravity.y is: moSHion works out how fast the jump has to start. Change any of them later: player.maxJumps = 3 after a power-up, and player.jumpsLeft = player.maxJumps gives every jump back, even in mid-air.","code":"let player, platforms;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 10;\n\n  player = new Sprite(200, 300, 30, 40);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n  player.rotationLock = true;\n  player.maxJumps = 2;\n\n  platforms = new Group();\n  platforms.collider = 'static';\n  platforms.color = 'slategray';\n  new platforms.Sprite(200, 390, 400, 20);\n  new platforms.Sprite(110, 260, 100, 12);\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left')) {\n    player.vel.x = -4;\n  } else if (kb.pressing('right')) {\n    player.vel.x = 4;\n  } else {\n    player.vel.x = 0;\n  }\n\n  if (kb.presses(' ')) {\n    player.jump(platforms);\n  }\n\n  if (player.onGround) {\n    player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n  } else {\n    player.color = 'orange';\n  }\n}\n\nfunction drawTop() {\n  fill('white');\n  text('Jumps left: ' + player.jumpsLeft, 12, 24);\n}"},{"title":"Projectiles from a player","body":"Keep bullets in a Group so they share defaults and are easy to iterate. Setting group defaults (color, diameter, collider) once saves repeating yourself each spawn.\n\nSpawn a new bullet with new bullets.Sprite(x, y) when the fire key is pressed. Give it a velocity so it flies across the screen.\n\nRemove bullets once they leave the canvas — otherwise they pile up and slow the sketch down. Iterate a copy with [...bullets] so removing doesn't break the loop.","code":"let player, bullets;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  player = new Sprite(60, 200, 30, 30);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n\n  bullets = new Group();\n  bullets.diameter = 10;\n  bullets.color = 'gold';\n  bullets.collider = 'none';\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('up'))        player.vel.y = -4;\n  else if (kb.pressing('down')) player.vel.y = 4;\n  else                          player.vel.y = 0;\n\n  if (kb.presses(' ')) {\n    const b = new bullets.Sprite(player.x + 20, player.y);\n    b.vel.x = 8;\n  }\n\n  for (const b of [...bullets]) {\n    if (b.x > 420) b.delete();\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n}"},{"title":"Score and timer HUD","body":"Use plain variables to track game state: one for score, one for the remaining time. Increase the score when the player overlaps a collectible, and remove the collectible so it doesn't re-trigger.\n\nConvert frameCount into seconds by dividing by 60 (the default frame rate). Math.max(0, ...) stops the timer clamping into negative numbers.\n\nDraw the HUD in draw() after background() so it sits on top. Use fixed x/y coordinates so the HUD stays put if the camera scrolls.","code":"let player, coins;\nlet score = 0;\nconst LIMIT = 20;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  player = new Sprite(200, 200, 30, 30);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n\n  coins = new Group();\n  coins.diameter = 18;\n  coins.color = 'gold';\n  coins.collider = 'none';\n  for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) {\n    new coins.Sprite(40 + i * 60, 80 + (i % 2) * 200);\n  }\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -4;\n  else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 4;\n  else                           player.vel.x = 0;\n  if (kb.pressing('up'))         player.vel.y = -4;\n  else if (kb.pressing('down'))  player.vel.y = 4;\n  else                           player.vel.y = 0;\n\n  for (const c of [...coins]) {\n    if (player.overlapping(c)) {\n      score++;\n      c.delete();\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  const timeLeft = Math.max(0, LIMIT - Math.floor(frameCount / 60));\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(18);\n  text('Score: ' + score, 14, 26);\n  text('Time: ' + timeLeft, 14, 50);\n}"},{"title":"Timers: every and after","body":"every(frames, fn) calls a function every so many frames, and after(frames, fn) calls it once, that many frames from now. At 60 frames a second, every(60, spawn) runs spawn about once a second.\n\nThey count frames, not seconds, so they stay in step with frameCount: every(60, spawn) made in setup() fires on exactly the frames where frameCount % 60 === 0 is true. They pause when the sketch stops with noLoop().\n\nBoth give back a timer you can keep in a variable and stop() later: const t = every(30, blink); ... t.stop(); timer.active tells you whether it is still going (an after() timer stops being active once it has fired).","code":"let pipes;\nlet spawner;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 300);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  pipes = new Group();\n  pipes.color = 'limegreen';\n  pipes.collider = 'none';\n\n  spawner = every(45, () => {\n    const p = new pipes.Sprite(420, 40 + Math.random() * 220, 30, 80);\n    p.vel.x = -3;\n  });\n\n  after(600, () => {\n    spawner.stop();\n  });\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  pipes.cull(60);\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(14);\n  text(spawner.active ? 'spawning' : 'stopped', 14, 20);\n}"},{"title":"Scene/state switching","body":"A single state variable is enough for most small games. Use strings like 'menu', 'play', and 'gameover' — they're easy to read in if/else branches.\n\nIn update(), branch on state to handle input for that scene only. In draw(), branch on state to render the right screen. Changing state is just state = 'play'.\n\nKeep scene-specific sprites hidden or disabled when not in that scene. The simplest approach is to only create them when entering 'play' and remove them when leaving.","code":"let state = 'menu';\nlet player;\n\nfunction setup() {\n  new Canvas(400, 400);\n  world.gravity.y = 0;\n  player = new Sprite(200, 200, 30, 30);\n  player.color = 'deepskyblue';\n  player.visible = false;\n}\n\nfunction update() {\n  if (state === 'menu' && kb.presses(' ')) {\n    state = 'play';\n    player.visible = true;\n  } else if (state === 'play') {\n    if (kb.pressing('left'))       player.vel.x = -4;\n    else if (kb.pressing('right')) player.vel.x = 4;\n    else                           player.vel.x = 0;\n    if (kb.presses('x')) state = 'gameover';\n  } else if (state === 'gameover' && kb.presses(' ')) {\n    state = 'menu';\n    player.visible = false;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction draw() {\n  background('#222');\n  fill('white');\n  textSize(20);\n  if (state === 'menu') {\n    text('Press SPACE to start', 90, 200);\n  } else if (state === 'play') {\n    text('Press X to end', 14, 24);\n  } else {\n    text('Game Over', 140, 180);\n    text('SPACE for menu', 115, 220);\n  }\n}"}]}];
